dth: 1.5%;"></div><div class="liq-rule edge" style="top: 7.65306px;"></div><div class="liq-rule edge" style="top: 96.4286px;"></div><div class="liq-rule poc" style="top: 40.5612px;"></div><div class="liq-rule" style="top: 75.3061px;"></div><div class="liq-px" style="top: 75.3061px;"></div><div class="liq-tag" style="top: 7.65306px;"><b>7,827.5</b>Value high</div><div class="liq-tag poc" style="top: 40.5612px;"><b>7,772.5</b>Point of control</div><div class="liq-tag px" style="top: 75.3061px;"><b>7,717.0</b>Price now</div><div class="liq-tag" style="top: 101.306px;"><b>7,682.5</b>Value low</div><div class="liq-rail"><div class="liq-pm" title="7,708.8 · sell stops below this level" style="top: 80.3204px; width: 13px;"></div><div class="liq-pm" title="7,738.1 · buy stops above this level" style="top: 62.3755px; width: 13px;"></div><div class="liq-pm" title="7,686.3 · sell stops below this level" style="top: 94.1327px; width: 13px;"></div><div class="liq-pm" title="7,764.4 · buy stops above this level" style="top: 46.3102px; width: 13px;"></div><div class="liq-pm" title="7,667.7 · sell stops below this level" style="top: 105.496px; width: 13px;"></div><div class="liq-pm" title="7,784.2 · buy stops above this level" style="top: 34.1388px; width: 13px;"></div></div></div></div><div class="rt-key"><details class="fwork liq-f liq-rd" data-t="liq-leg"><summary>Built from <b>5510</b> five-minute bars covering <b>679.6 hours</b>. Blue is the value area — the band holding 70% of all volume in the window.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The point of control is the single busiest price in it. Bar length is contracts traded at that price, nothing else.</div></details></div><div class="rt-key"><b>Cash session only</b> (1530 bars): value <b>7,685.0–7,827.5</b>, point of control <b>7,772.5</b>. That is the same price the all-hours profile picks, so the overnight trade is not distorting the structure here.<details class="fwork liq-f" data-t="liq-w8"><summary>Turns are <b>weighted</b> by the liquidity present when they happened, so a quiet overnight turn does not count the same as one at the bell.</summary><div class="fwork-b">A turn in the cash session counts <b>1.00</b>, one in the European morning <b>0.36</b>, one in Asian hours <b>0.21</b>. Those weights are measured from this window's own volume, not assumed.</div></details></div><div class="liq-call"><div class="liq-lean">Rotational — the pull is toward 7,708.8</div><details class="fwork liq-f liq-w" data-t="liq-why"><summary>Price is <b>inside value</b>, the band where most of the window's volume traded.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Inside value, moves tend to rotate rather than run, and the point of control at <b>7,772.5</b> acts as the magnet (8.2× 30m away). Untouched stops sit <b>7,738.1</b> above (3.1× 30m) and <b>7,708.8</b> below (1.2× 30m). Price reaches for the nearer pool more often than the far one.</div></details></div><div class="invalid"><b>What breaks it —</b> This read is wrong the moment price closes outside value — below <b>7,682.5</b> or above <b>7,827.5</b>. Outside value the rotational logic stops applying and it becomes a trend read.</div><div class="sectlabel">Where stops are likely resting — nearest first</div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,708.8</div><div class="pd">-8.2</div><div class="pd">1.2× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Sell stops below</b> this level<br>41 turns (19A+13EU+6RTH+3EV)<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 2h ago</span><br><span class="pmeta">~3h away</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,738.1</div><div class="pd">+21.1</div><div class="pd">3.1× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Buy stops above</b> this level<br>88 turns (49A+21EU+16RTH+2EV) · 24h extreme<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 8h ago</span><br><span class="pmeta">~16h away</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,686.3</div><div class="pd">-30.8</div><div class="pd">4.6× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Sell stops below</b> this level<br>76 turns (26RTH+25EU+20A+5EV)<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 12h ago</span><br><span class="pmeta">needs the cash session — it opens in 5.6h</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,764.4</div><div class="pd">+47.4</div><div class="pd">7.0× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Buy stops above</b> this level<br>113 turns (55A+28EU+27RTH+3EV)<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 7.7d ago — stale</span><br><span class="pmeta">needs the cash session — it opens in 5.6h</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,667.7</div><div class="pd">-49.3</div><div class="pd">7.3× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Sell stops below</b> this level<br>67 turns (34A+17RTH+11EU+5EV) · 24h extreme<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 1.7d ago — stale</span><br><span class="pmeta">needs the cash session — it opens in 5.6h</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="liq-pool"><div><div class="pp">7,784.2</div><div class="pd">+67.2</div><div class="pd">10.0× 30m</div></div><div class="pn"><b>Buy stops above</b> this level<br>104 turns (38A+33RTH+29EU+4EV)<br><span class="pmeta">last touched 9.6d ago — stale</span><br><span class="pmeta">needs the cash session — it opens in 5.6h</span></div><div class="pw"><u><i class="on" style="height:5px"></i><i class="on" style="height:8px"></i><i class="on" style="height:11px"></i></u>heavy</div></div><div class="rt-key"><details class="fwork liq-f" data-t="liq-meth"><summary>Stop pools are <b>inferred</b>, not measured. Turn counts are by session — <b>RTH</b> US cash, <b>EU</b> European, <b>EV</b> evening, <b>A</b> Asian.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The <b>×</b> figures are distances in typical 30-minute moves. The divisor is the measured pace of <b>the session the clock is in</b>, not an all-hours average: ES does not move at one speed, and a median that blends a dead overnight hour with the New York open describes neither of them. Right now that is <b>European hours</b>, where one move is <b>6.8 pt</b> (median of 1465 clean half-hour windows in the 680-hour archive). At the bell that median becomes <b>11.0 pt</b>, so every × here divides by about <b>1.6×</b> when the cash session opens. The distance does not change; the reach does. Under <b>1×</b> is inside normal reach for the next half hour; past <b>2×</b> needs a fresh catalyst or a change of session. Whether <i>today</i> is running at that pace is a separate question and the decision board answers it, by checking the last hour's realised travel against what this session normally does. There is no order-book or depth data in this feed — these are levels obvious enough (repeated turns, session extremes, round numbers) that most desks would rest stops beyond them, and price has not yet traded through them. The session split matters because a level turned six times in Asian trade and one turned three times at the New York open are not the same level, however similar they look on a chart. Treat a heavy pool as somewhere price is <i>drawn to</i>, not as a level that holds.</div></details></div></div>
    <!-- Contracts on the relay with no profile on this map, and why. Written by
         liquidity2 into LIQ_EXCL, printed unaltered. The decision board reads the
         SAME object rather than carrying its own wording -- see liqExclHtml. -->
    <div class="liq-excl" id="liqExcl"><b>Not on this map:</b> HSI — no volume at all. The relay carries HSI as the CASH INDEX, and an index has no volume at any price — there is nothing to weight a profile with. All 1,090 bars of it print a volume of zero, so every figure this card exists to produce would be a statement about an empty histogram: a point of control chosen by tie-break, a value area covering the whole range, and stop pools at turns that no contracts were traded through. Those would not be weak numbers, they would be numbers about nothing, and they would look exactly like the real ones. The tradeable instrument is the Mini-Hang Seng future, whose profile IS measured on real transacted volume — it is the Hong Kong board on the level ladder, which carries its own volume and its own pull date.</div>
    <button class="mini" type="button" id="liqTvBtn" style="margin-top:12px">Show table view</button>
    <div class="tvwrap" id="liqTvWrap"><table class="tvbox" id="liqTv"><thead><tr><th>Price</th><th>What it is</th><th>Dist</th><th>Age</th><th>Weight</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7,827.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Value area high</td><td>+110.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,772.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Point of control (busiest price)</td><td>+55.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,682.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Value area low</td><td>-34.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,827.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Value area high — cash session only</td><td>+110.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,772.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Point of control — cash session only</td><td>+55.5</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,685.0</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Value area low — cash session only</td><td>-32.0</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>7,708.8</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Sell stops below — 41 turns (19A+13EU+6RTH+3EV)</td><td>-8.2</td><td>2h</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>7,738.1</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Buy stops above — 88 turns (49A+21EU+16RTH+2EV), 24h extreme</td><td>+21.1</td><td>8h</td><td>73</td></tr><tr><td>7,686.3</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Sell stops below — 76 turns (26RTH+25EU+20A+5EV)</td><td>-30.8</td><td>12h</td><td>84</td></tr><tr><td>7,764.4</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Buy stops above — 113 turns (55A+28EU+27RTH+3EV), stale</td><td>+47.4</td><td>7.7d</td><td>76</td></tr><tr><td>7,667.7</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Sell stops below — 67 turns (34A+17RTH+11EU+5EV), 24h extreme, stale</td><td>-49.3</td><td>1.7d</td><td>49</td></tr><tr><td>7,784.2</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Buy stops above — 104 turns (38A+33RTH+29EU+4EV), stale</td><td>+67.2</td><td>9.6d</td><td>81</td></tr><tr><td>7,636.4</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Sell stops below — 24 turns (12A+10EU+1RTH+1EV), stale</td><td>-80.6</td><td>22.9d</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>7,806.0</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Buy stops above — 39 turns (15A+14RTH+9EU+1EV), stale</td><td>+89.0</td><td>9.8d</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>7,824.5</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Buy stops above — 51 turns (24EU+20A+6RTH+1EV), round number, stale</td><td>+107.5</td><td>9.9d</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>7,552.4</td><td style="text-align:left;white-space:normal">Sell stops below — 18 turns (9A+8EU+1RTH), round number, stale</td><td>-164.6</td><td>23.8d</td><td>10</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CATALYST BOARD -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,RTY" id="rail-catalyst-board">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Catalyst board</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>What is coming, and whether it can reach anything that matters. </summary><div class="fwork-b">The bar under each event is a <b>blast radius</b>: the current price at the centre, and the distance the event would have to travel to run each stop pool on the liquidity map. Its width is your <b>measured</b> 30-minute range multiplied by a <b>judged</b> factor for the event — the first number comes from the bars, the second is my estimate and is the part to distrust. Headlines are as fresh as the last brief, not streaming.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div id="catBody"><div class="smt-when smt-gen">measured 13 Aug 08:24 UTC · 13 days ago <span class="dim">as measured</span> <span class="dim">· carried forward: the last refresh could not regenerate this card</span></div><div class="cat-hd"><div class="ch-lab">Calendar</div><div class="ch-fig">Nothing scheduled</div><div class="ch-note">Every catalyst on this board has passed. This page has not reached the calendar feed, so it is showing the list baked in at the last brief — which has now run out. Open it in a browser and it refills itself. Treat moves as flow rather than news.</div></div><div class="sectlabel">What has landed</div><div class="ld-none">Scheduled events that have already printed, newest first, with what the tape did afterwards measured against every ordinary window of the same length. <b>Green means the tape agreed with what the result argued for</b> — never that it went up. Direction is the arrow; the bar is how unusual the move was. <b>9 of the 9 below are still waiting on their actual.</b></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How a missing actual gets filled in</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>The relay’s calendar carries a forecast and a prior and no result, so this page recovers them a week later from the next occurrence’s prior field, or sooner by hand. Until then those rows report the move and score nothing.</p></div></details><div class="ld-wrap"><div class="ld-row"><div class="ld-top"><div class="ld-nm">GDP m/m</div><div class="ld-when">13 Aug 14:00</div></div><div><span class="ld-chip none">no actual yet</span></div><div class="ld-lead">The tape went down: <b>ES -0.75</b> over the 42 minutes bracketing the print, bigger than <b>16%</b> of windows that long.</div><div><div class="ld-none">Consensus <b>-0.1%</b>, prior <b>0.1%</b> — <b>no actual yet</b>.</div></div><div><div class="ld-arms"><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">ES</span><span class="d dn">▼</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil dn" style="width:16%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">16</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">NQ</span><span class="d dn">▼</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil dn" style="width:15%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">15</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">RTY</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:9%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">9</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">YM</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:35%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">35</span></div></div></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How this was measured, and what it cannot say</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p><b>Where the numbers come from.</b> The move is measured on the quote archive, and every timestamp in it has the provider delay taken out. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price that was true about 10 minutes earlier, so an event read off its row label would have its whole "reaction" measured before the print. That correction is the single thing this section depends on being right.</p><p><b>The window is 42 minutes, not thirty.</b> The archive steps every six, so the last row before a print can be a step early and the first row past the half-hour a step late. Rather than pretend to a round number, the base rate below is computed at the span actually measured — a longer window compared against a thirty-minute yardstick would flatter every reaction on this board.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>30m</th><th>vs typical</th><th>pct</th><th>2h</th><th>pct</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>-0.75</td><td>0.50×</td><td>16</td><td>-0.75</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>-6.25</td><td>0.58×</td><td>15</td><td>-38.00</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>+0.20</td><td>0.29×</td><td>9</td><td>+0.30</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>YM</td><td>+13.00</td><td>1.44×</td><td>35</td><td>+35.00</td><td>44</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Read the percentile, not the direction.</b> Over this archive ES rose in <b>57%</b> of all 42-minute windows — so "it went up after the print" is close to no information at all. The percentile is what carries the claim: it says where this move sits among the <b>2182</b> ordinary windows of the same length, whose median move was <b>3.25</b>. Anything under the 75th is reported as noise here however tidily it points the right way.</p><p><b>The two-hour column is not attribution.</b> At two hours the release is one of many things that happened, and this board has no way to separate them. It is there because a reaction that is given straight back is a different event from one that holds — not because the release owns it.</p><p><b>Which way a beat argues is a judgement, and it is dated.</b> This release carries no mark, so nothing here is scored. That mapping rests on the regime this tape is in — pricing a hike rather than a cut — and it would flip with the regime. It is also <b>first order only</b>: mild labour weakness is rate relief and severe labour weakness is a growth scare, and they point opposite ways, so a monotone sign is wrong in the tails by construction. A row scored <i>against</i> is as likely to be this map failing as the market misbehaving.</p></div></details></div><div class="ld-row"><div class="ld-top"><div class="ld-nm">Prelim GDP q/q</div><div class="ld-when">13 Aug 14:00</div></div><div><span class="ld-chip none">no actual yet</span></div><div class="ld-lead">The second release of a simultaneous print cluster: it landed and the reaction — one tape move for the whole cluster — is measured once, under <b>GDP m/m</b> above.</div><div><div class="ld-none">Consensus <b>0.4%</b>, prior <b>0.6%</b> — <b>no actual yet</b>.</div></div><div><div class="ld-arms"><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">ES</span><span class="d dn">▼</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil dn" style="width:16%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">16</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">NQ</span><span class="d dn">▼</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil dn" style="width:15%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">15</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">RTY</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:9%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">9</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">YM</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:35%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">35</span></div></div></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How this was measured, and what it cannot say</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p><b>Where the numbers come from.</b> The move is measured on the quote archive, and every timestamp in it has the provider delay taken out. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price that was true about 10 minutes earlier, so an event read off its row label would have its whole "reaction" measured before the print. That correction is the single thing this section depends on being right.</p><p><b>The window is 42 minutes, not thirty.</b> The archive steps every six, so the last row before a print can be a step early and the first row past the half-hour a step late. Rather than pretend to a round number, the base rate below is computed at the span actually measured — a longer window compared against a thirty-minute yardstick would flatter every reaction on this board.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>30m</th><th>vs typical</th><th>pct</th><th>2h</th><th>pct</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>-0.75</td><td>0.50×</td><td>16</td><td>-0.75</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>-6.25</td><td>0.58×</td><td>15</td><td>-38.00</td><td>39</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>+0.20</td><td>0.29×</td><td>9</td><td>+0.30</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>YM</td><td>+13.00</td><td>1.44×</td><td>35</td><td>+35.00</td><td>44</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Read the percentile, not the direction.</b> Over this archive ES rose in <b>57%</b> of all 42-minute windows — so "it went up after the print" is close to no information at all. The percentile is what carries the claim: it says where this move sits among the <b>2182</b> ordinary windows of the same length, whose median move was <b>3.25</b>. Anything under the 75th is reported as noise here however tidily it points the right way.</p><p><b>The two-hour column is not attribution.</b> At two hours the release is one of many things that happened, and this board has no way to separate them. It is there because a reaction that is given straight back is a different event from one that holds — not because the release owns it.</p><p><b>Which way a beat argues is a judgement, and it is dated.</b> This release is marked <b>cyclical</b>: an actual above consensus argues the index <b>up</b>. That mapping rests on the regime this tape is in — pricing a hike rather than a cut — and it would flip with the regime. It is also <b>first order only</b>: mild labour weakness is rate relief and severe labour weakness is a growth scare, and they point opposite ways, so a monotone sign is wrong in the tails by construction. A row scored <i>against</i> is as likely to be this map failing as the market misbehaving.</p></div></details></div><div class="ld-row"><div class="ld-top"><div class="ld-nm">CPI m/m</div><div class="ld-when">12 Aug 20:30</div></div><div><span class="ld-chip none">no actual yet</span></div><div class="ld-lead">The tape went up: <b>ES +13.75</b> over the 42 minutes bracketing the print, bigger than <b>92%</b> of windows that long.</div><div><div class="ld-none">Consensus <b>0.1%</b>, prior <b>-0.4%</b> — <b>no actual yet</b>.</div></div><div><div class="ld-arms"><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">ES</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:92%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">92</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">NQ</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:92%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">92</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">RTY</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:92%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">92</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">YM</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:61%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">61</span></div></div></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How this was measured, and what it cannot say</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p><b>Where the numbers come from.</b> The move is measured on the quote archive, and every timestamp in it has the provider delay taken out. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price that was true about 10 minutes earlier, so an event read off its row label would have its whole "reaction" measured before the print. That correction is the single thing this section depends on being right.</p><p><b>The window is 42 minutes, not thirty.</b> The archive steps every six, so the last row before a print can be a step early and the first row past the half-hour a step late. Rather than pretend to a round number, the base rate below is computed at the span actually measured — a longer window 