div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">YM</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:89%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">89</span></div></div></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How this was measured, and what it cannot say</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p><b>Where the numbers come from.</b> The move is measured on the quote archive, and every timestamp in it has the provider delay taken out. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price that was true about 10 minutes earlier, so an event read off its row label would have its whole "reaction" measured before the print. That correction is the single thing this section depends on being right.</p><p><b>The window is 42 minutes, not thirty.</b> The archive steps every six, so the last row before a print can be a step early and the first row past the half-hour a step late. Rather than pretend to a round number, the base rate below is computed at the span actually measured — a longer window compared against a thirty-minute yardstick would flatter every reaction on this board.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>30m</th><th>vs typical</th><th>pct</th><th>2h</th><th>pct</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>+15.25</td><td>10.17×</td><td>94</td><td>+20.00</td><td>89</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>+149.25</td><td>13.88×</td><td>97</td><td>+73.50</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>+10.40</td><td>14.85×</td><td>97</td><td>+12.60</td><td>92</td></tr><tr><td>YM</td><td>+76.00</td><td>8.44×</td><td>89</td><td>+96.00</td><td>80</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Read the percentile, not the direction.</b> Over this archive ES rose in <b>57%</b> of all 42-minute windows — so "it went up after the print" is close to no information at all. The percentile is what carries the claim: it says where this move sits among the <b>2182</b> ordinary windows of the same length, whose median move was <b>3.25</b>. Anything under the 75th is reported as noise here however tidily it points the right way.</p><p><b>The two-hour column is not attribution.</b> At two hours the release is one of many things that happened, and this board has no way to separate them. It is there because a reaction that is given straight back is a different event from one that holds — not because the release owns it.</p><p><b>Which way a beat argues is a judgement, and it is dated.</b> This release is marked <b>hawkish</b>: an actual above consensus argues the index <b>down</b>. That mapping rests on the regime this tape is in — pricing a hike rather than a cut — and it would flip with the regime. It is also <b>first order only</b>: mild labour weakness is rate relief and severe labour weakness is a growth scare, and they point opposite ways, so a monotone sign is wrong in the tails by construction. A row scored <i>against</i> is as likely to be this map failing as the market misbehaving.</p></div></details></div><div class="ld-row"><div class="ld-top"><div class="ld-nm">Unemployment Rate</div><div class="ld-when">7 Aug 20:30</div></div><div><span class="ld-chip none">no actual yet</span></div><div class="ld-lead">The third release of a simultaneous print cluster: it landed and the reaction — one tape move for the whole cluster — is measured once, under <b>Average Hourly Earnings m/m</b> above.</div><div><div class="ld-none">Consensus <b>4.2%</b>, prior <b>4.2%</b> — <b>no actual yet</b>.</div></div><div><div class="ld-arms"><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">ES</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:94%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">94</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">NQ</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:97%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">97</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">RTY</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:97%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">97</span></div><div class="ld-arm"><span class="s">YM</span><span class="d up">▲</span><span class="ld-trk" aria-hidden="true"><span class="ld-fil up" style="width:89%"></span><span class="ld-ref" style="left:75%"></span></span><span class="pc">89</span></div></div></div><details class="disc"><summary><span>How this was measured, and what it cannot say</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p><b>Where the numbers come from.</b> The move is measured on the quote archive, and every timestamp in it has the provider delay taken out. A row labelled 12:18 carries an ES price that was true about 10 minutes earlier, so an event read off its row label would have its whole "reaction" measured before the print. That correction is the single thing this section depends on being right.</p><p><b>The window is 42 minutes, not thirty.</b> The archive steps every six, so the last row before a print can be a step early and the first row past the half-hour a step late. Rather than pretend to a round number, the base rate below is computed at the span actually measured — a longer window compared against a thirty-minute yardstick would flatter every reaction on this board.</p><table class="disc-tbl"><tbody><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>30m</th><th>vs typical</th><th>pct</th><th>2h</th><th>pct</th></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>+15.25</td><td>10.17×</td><td>94</td><td>+20.00</td><td>89</td></tr><tr><td>NQ</td><td>+149.25</td><td>13.88×</td><td>97</td><td>+73.50</td><td>67</td></tr><tr><td>RTY</td><td>+10.40</td><td>14.85×</td><td>97</td><td>+12.60</td><td>92</td></tr><tr><td>YM</td><td>+76.00</td><td>8.44×</td><td>89</td><td>+96.00</td><td>80</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Read the percentile, not the direction.</b> Over this archive ES rose in <b>57%</b> of all 42-minute windows — so "it went up after the print" is close to no information at all. The percentile is what carries the claim: it says where this move sits among the <b>2182</b> ordinary windows of the same length, whose median move was <b>3.25</b>. Anything under the 75th is reported as noise here however tidily it points the right way.</p><p><b>The two-hour column is not attribution.</b> At two hours the release is one of many things that happened, and this board has no way to separate them. It is there because a reaction that is given straight back is a different event from one that holds — not because the release owns it.</p><p><b>Which way a beat argues is a judgement, and it is dated.</b> This release is marked <b>cyclical</b>: an actual above consensus argues the index <b>up</b>. That mapping rests on the regime this tape is in — pricing a hike rather than a cut — and it would flip with the regime. It is also <b>first order only</b>: mild labour weakness is rate relief and severe labour weakness is a growth scare, and they point opposite ways, so a monotone sign is wrong in the tails by construction. A row scored <i>against</i> is as likely to be this map failing as the market misbehaving.</p></div></details></div></div><div class="sectlabel">Stories behind the tone</div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">The island broke upward and then stopped — and the small cap is second-strongest</div><div class="nm2">Tape · 24d ago</div><div class="nw">RTY +0.732%, NQ +0.858%, ES +0.602%, YM +0.553%.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>Twelve basis points now separate the Russell contract from the Nasdaq, where Friday's cash session put a hundred and eleven between them, and the ORDER has inverted: the only index that fell on Friday is the second-strongest thing on the board tonight. The ranges were tight — ES 23.5 points, 0.31% of price; RTY 13.5 points, 0.46% — and both contracts have taken out the top of them. On Friday this page called that shape an island and published the test in the same sentence: an island resolves on volume rather than on time. It has resolved on time. The last half-hour covered 2.3 points on RTY and 5.75 on ES against measured 30-minute ranges of 5.4 and 10.5, so the break happened and the tape then went to sleep on it, seven hours before anybody who can ratify it turns up. A breakout nobody has traded is a level, not a verdict.</p></div></details></div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">WTI has lost 80 as well — the war premium is not coming back this week</div><div class="nm2">Commodities · 24d ago</div><div class="nw">Crude is below the 81.51 pre-strike floor this page uses as its own trigger, and has held below it since Sunday.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>That level was named a week ago as the price at which the Iran risk premium stops mattering for headline inflation; it was tested on Friday and HELD, and crude spent the rest of Friday going the other way, 81.45 in the morning to 85.85 by mid-afternoon and 84.57 at the 21:00Z close. It went on the Sunday reopen instead — 84.67 to 79.55 inside six minutes, straight through the floor and through 81 and 80 without trading at any of them. Nothing has printed above 81.25 since, so there are three and a half dollars of untraded air overhead: a retrace up into it carries no supply and means nothing until it reaches 84.67. It removes the one input that was putting a floor under headline CPI just as the Fed's hawkish minority was looking for a reason, and it does so in the week of a payrolls print. It also sharpens Monday's ISM prices-paid: with no commodity floor left, a hot print is a domestic price-pressure read rather than an energy pass-through, which is the more worrying of the two. Note the mechanical consequence for the card below: the oil component of the sentiment score is pinned at its positive rail, so it has stopped discriminating. Read it as a state, not as a reading.</p></div></details></div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">One of the two frozen references has started rolling again — which is not the same as being fixed</div><div class="nm2">Data · 24d ago</div><div class="nw">HSI and WTI are the only two quotes on this relay that come from CNBC, and both had been carrying a prior close that stopped rolling forward after Thursday.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>WTI still is: it publishes −6.24% today against a session that has moved about three quarters of a per cent. HSI is not: its reference has rolled, and the session opened 0.40% above it, which is exactly what a working previous close looks like. HSI stays suppressed anyway, and the reason is the whole point of the list. The defect was never that a payload read wrong — it was that no single payload can tell a frozen reference from a correct one, because they are the same object. One payload that looks right is one observation of the thing that was already unobservable, and an intermittent fault is worse than a permanent one, because the readings that look fine are the ones that get traded. Clearing this needs the field watched across sessions, which is relay-side work, not page-side. Levels from both feeds remain usable; changes do not.</p></div></details></div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">The S&amp;P beat the Nasdaq while small caps fell — a rates session, not a tech one</div><div class="nm2">Tape · 26d ago</div><div class="nw">SPX +0.698%, NDX +0.593%, RUT −0.522%.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>That ranking is diagnostic and it is unusual. A tech story puts the Nasdaq on top; a risk-off session takes the broad index down with everything else. Here the broad index led, the growth index kept up, and the only index that fell is the one whose earnings are levered to the front end. This is the clean signature of a term-premium regime rather than an AI story, and it is the most useful thing Friday produced. The practical consequence is immediate: semiconductor headlines carried no daily information on Friday and should not be read this week as though they did.</p></div></details></div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">The 30-year closed at 5.27%, above the level being called a multidecade high two days earlier</div><div class="nm2">Rates · 26d ago</div><div class="nw">Treasury close: 2-year 4.28%, 5-year 4.45%, 10-year 4.75%, 30-year 5.27%.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>The 10-year added 8.2bp on the day and 2s10s steepened 2bp to +47bp — the long end doing the work, which is exactly what separates a term-premium move from an inflation move. This is the input that caps RTY and it did not improve. One caveat before anything is traded off it: the curve here is Friday's official Treasury close, while the futures on this page are live to the minute, so the two are not the same vintage and a comparison between them is not a spread.</p></div></details></div><div class="cat-news"><div class="nh">VIX fell 6.4% to 15.99 going into a payrolls week</div><div class="nm2">Volatility · 26d ago</div><div class="nw">Down from 17.09. A sixteen handle is the market declining to pay for protection ahead of the only genuinely binary event on the week's calendar, which is either confidence or complacency — and the tape does not distinguish between them in advance.</div><details class="disc"><summary><span>The working</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>What it does mean mechanically is that the options market is pricing a smaller move around Friday's print than the measured 30-minute ranges on this board imply, so the payrolls band on the catalyst card above is wider than what is currently being sold.</p></div></details></div></div>
    <button class="mini" type="button" id="catTvBtn" style="margin-top:12px">Show table view</button>
    <div class="tvwrap" id="catTvWrap"><table class="tvbox" id="catTv"><thead><tr><th>When (HKT)</th><th>Event</th><th>Hits</th><th>Impact</th><th>Band</th><th>In reach</th></tr></thead><tbody></tbody></table></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- LEVEL LADDER -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,RTY,NQ,YM" id="rail-level-ladder">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Level ladder</div>
    <div class="card-sub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>Every level in your map, in order, with state and true distance from price. </summary><div class="fwork-b">Tap a row for the history behind it. State is the level’s most recent verdict, not its polarity — <b>Held</b> means it did its job on the last test, whether it was acting as support or as resistance.</div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="ctl-row">
    <div class="tabs" id="ladderTabs" role="tablist"><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true">RTY</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">ES</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">MHI</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">NQ</button><button class="tab" type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false">YM</button></div>
    <div class="priceform">
      <label for="priceIn">Live px</label>
      <input id="priceIn" type="number" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="3007.4">
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div class="ladder" id="ladder"><div class="card-sub" style="margin-bottom: 2px;">Quoted in FUTURES, the same frame as the liquidity map below — mixing a cash ladder with a futures profile is how a level ends up eight ticks from where you think it is. The ratio on this board is 1.0024. It moved from 1.0027 on Thursday 6 August because the trigger written down here in advance on Wednesday fired, and it was executed without being re-argued under the fresh number — which was the entire purpose of writing it down.</div><details class="disc" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><summary><span>Why 1.0027 became 1.0024, and why the RULE is being replaced too · three modes and a real hollow · the invalid cross-check</span></summary><div class="disc-body"><p>THE RULE FIRED AND THE RULE IS ALSO WRONG. Wednesday’s 20:00:00Z bar opened RTY 3,024.60 against a settled cash RUT of 3,018.4221, a ratio of 1.002047. That is a fourth consecutive step in the same direction — 1.002818, 1.002714, 1.002446, 1.002047 — and one more step down was the stated trigger, so the constant moved to 1.0024 as written. Executing it is what exposed the defect in it. The rule moves the constant to the PREVIOUS observation, so 1.0024 was already 3.5e-4 stale on the morning it was adopted, and on RUT near 3,018 that is 1.07 of a one-point RTY bin: the board adopted a constant that was already outside the tolerance the trigger is defined by. The replacement rule, for the next move on either contract, is that the constant goes to the CURRENT measurement rounded to four decimals whenever the error exceeds one bin width — not to the last one, and not on a count of consecutive steps, because a count of steps measures the trend and the thing that matters is the error. ES stays at 1.0037 and goes on the watch list rather than moving: its error grew from 6.1e-5 to 3.16e-4 in one session, which is 2.44 ES points and 0.98 of a 2.5-point bin. That is AT the threshold and not through it, and a constant re-cut every time it moves is a constant that has become noise.</p><p>Basis RE-MEASURED at Tuesday’s 20:00:00Z cash close, anchored on the OPEN of the 20:00 bar rather than its close, because that bar’s close is already the 20:05 print: RTY 3,044.50 futures against cash RUT 3,037.07, a ratio of 1.002446. Three sessions read 1.002818, then 1.002713, then 1.002446 — a compression of 3.72e-4 across three days, and accelerating: −1.05e-4 then −2.67e-4. Against 1.0027 the error is now 2.54e-4, which is 0.77 of a point on RUT at 3,037. RTY bins are one point wide, so the error is approaching one bin and has not crossed it. Re-cutting a constant every time it moves is how a constant becomes noise; re-cutting it one session too late is how a board publishes a level in the wrong bin. The rule for Thursday is written down here in advance so it cannot be argued after the fact: one more step in the same direction and the ratio moves to 1.0024. A step back toward 1.0027 and it stays.</p><p>A second estimator built from what this board called same-instant medians read 1.00248, 1.00290, 1.00276 across those three sessions and did not corroborate the trend. Two things were wrong with it and only one was known. The known one: its final reading covered ninety-nine minutes of Tuesday, because the squash truncated the quote history. The unknown one is worse, and is now measured — the legs were never same-instant. Each row of the quote archive carries one timestamp and thirteen prices at thirteen different provider delays, and here the error runs the opposite way to ES: RTY arrives 602 seconds old against cash RUT at 1,022, so this estimator was dividing a stale futures price into a staler cash one. Shifting RUT to the matching true instant walks it onto the settle-anchored number almost exactly — 3 August reads 1.002694 at a one-row shift against a bar-open 1.002714, and 4 August 1.002426 at two rows against 1.002446. The two days land at different shifts because the true offset is about one and a half rows and the archive steps only in whole ones. So the cross-check was never disagreeing with the bar-open method: it was measuring a different pair of instants and reporting the gap as a basis. It is withdrawn as evidence in both directions. Leaving 1.0027 alone rests on the three settle-anchored readings and on bin width, which is where it already rested.</p><p>Every level below is rebuilt from the same 5,511-bar, 679.6-hour, one-point-bin profile and the same session-weighted stop model the liquidity map publishes, so the two cards cannot quietly disagree. One thing this rebuild found that the last one could not say: the 70% value area is 3,001.0 to 3,063.0 and the market is not in it. 70.68% of the window traded at or beneath 2,987. The board is now describing three modes — an old value area, a hollow, and a new shelf — and the hollow is real rather than a gap in the sample.</p><p>On the reach model, the grades held their signs for a second session, which is weak evidence that they describe the model rather than only the last window: cash-hour error +12% on 126 windows against +18% last time, Asian +18% against +23%. Positive means generous, so RTY’s excursion estimates still want discounting. European hours read −21%, in the other direction, and that split is not explained. The price box takes the tape rather than a hand-typed settlement; type over it to pin the board to your own fill.</p></div></details><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,079.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--text-muted)">○</i>Untested</span><div class="lname">Window high</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:69.92187499999984%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+71.6<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+2.38%</span><span class="lmove">18.4× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">The top of the 679.6-hour window on 0.01% of window volume. Nothing above this has ever traded inside the measurement, so the correct output above it is UNMAPPED rather than clear air.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,069.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--text-muted)">○</i>Untested</span><div class="lname">The best bid in the fourteen-bin void</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:60.15624999999986%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+61.6<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+2.05%</span><span class="lmove">15.8× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">The heaviest bin in a fourteen-bin void running 3,066.0 to 3,079.0 — all of those bins together hold 4.39% of everything traded, an average of 0.31% each. This one carries 0.54% of window volume, and that is the most a retrace has to catch on anywhere inside the stretch.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,065.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The ceiling of cash-session value · 30× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:56.249999999999865%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+57.6<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+1.92%</span><span class="lmove">14.8× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Edge of the 70% value area computed on cash-session bars only, on 0.73% of window volume of the all-hours profile. The cash crowd and the twenty-four-hour tape do not agree on where value is, and this is one end of the cash answer.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,063.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">Value-area high</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:54.296874999999865%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+55.6<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+1.85%</span><span class="lmove">14.3× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Upper edge of the 70% value area across the whole window, on 0.83% of window volume. 94.02% of everything traded in the window happened at or below this price, so it is the boundary value has reached rather than a shelf the market built.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,057.1</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The 102-turn Asian cluster · 102× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:48.496093749999815%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+49.7<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+1.65%</span><span class="lmove">12.7× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">102 turns — forty-six Asian, thirty-seven European, seventeen in the US cash session and two in the evening — scored 64 on 0.77% of window volume, and the third-heaviest pool on this board. Stale at about 184 hours. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a cash session at an excursion multiple of 22.6.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,038.5</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The eighty-six-turn cash-made cluster · 86× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:30.410156249999847%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+31.1<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+1.04%</span><span class="lmove">8.0× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Eighty-six turns — thirty-three in the US cash session, thirty-one Asian, eighteen European and four in the evening — scored 74 on 1.39% of window volume, and the heaviest pool on this board. Stale at about 165 hours. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a cash session at an excursion multiple of 14.2.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,029.5</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The sixty-nine-turn Asian cluster · 69× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:21.56249999999991%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+22.1<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+0.73%</span><span class="lmove">5.7× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Sixty-nine turns — thirty-four Asian, twenty-two European, nine in the US cash session and four in the evening — scored 42 on 1.15% of window volume. Stale at about 164 hours. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a cash session at an excursion multiple of 10.0.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,022.1</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The seventy-six-turn European cluster · 76× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:14.335937499999826%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+14.7<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+0.49%</span><span class="lmove">3.8× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Seventy-six turns — thirty-one European, twenty-five in the US cash session, eighteen Asian and two in the evening — scored 66 on 1.19% of window volume, and the second-heaviest pool on this board. Stale at about forty-three hours. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a cash session at an excursion multiple of 6.7.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,013.6</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--warning)">◆</i>Swept</span><div class="lname">The forty-eight-turn Asian cluster · 48× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:6.025390625000066%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+6.2<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+0.21%</span><span class="lmove">1.6× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Forty-eight turns — twenty Asian, twelve in the US cash session, twelve European and four in the evening — scored 48 on 0.87% of window volume. Set about five hours before the snapshot. The model calls its side above and grades it reachable inside a session at an excursion multiple of 2.8.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,008.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">POC — the magnet</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:2%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">+0.6<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">+0.02%</span><span class="lmove near">0.2× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Point of control: more contracts changed hands in this 1-point bin than in any other across 679.6 hours, 1.71% of window volume. The eleven bins around it hold 13.55% of everything traded between them, so this is a neighbourhood rather than a line.</div><div class="lrow here"><div class="lprice" style="color:var(--accent)">3,007.4</div><div class="lmid"><div class="youare">▸ You are here</div><div class="lname">Snapshot price, Thu, 27 Aug, 15:57 HKT — type over it to use your live print</div></div><div class="ldist" style="color:var(--accent)">—</div></div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,004.3</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">The seventy-eight-turn Asian cluster · 78× tested</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:3.066406249999873%;background:var(--ord-5)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">-3.1<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">-0.10%</span><span class="lmove near">0.8× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Seventy-eight turns — forty Asian, twenty-eight in the US cash session, eight European and two in the evening — scored 63 on 0.83% of window volume, and the fourth-heaviest pool on this board. Stale at about fifteen hours. The model calls its side below and grades it reachable inside a session at an excursion multiple of 1.4.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">3,001.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">●</i>Held</span><div class="lname">Value-area low</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:6.2500000000000835%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">-6.4<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">-0.21%</span><span class="lmove">1.6× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">Lower edge of the 70% value area across the whole window, on 0.75% of window volume, with 76.14% of everything traded at or above it. Thin, which is what makes it a boundary rather than a shelf.</div><div class="lrow"><div class="lprice">2,905.0</div><div class="lmid"><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--text-muted)">○</i>Untested</span><div class="lname">Window low</div><div class="barwrap"><div class="bar" style="width:100%;background:var(--ord-1)"></div></div></div><div class="ldist">-102.4<br><span style="color:var(--text-muted);font-size:10.5px">-3.40%</span><span class="lmove">26.3× 30m</span></div></div><div class="detail">The floor of the 679.6-hour window on 0.03% of window volume. A market that spends six days advancing never revisits its floor, so nothing here has been tested recently enough to trust.</div><div class="rt-key">The <b>×</b> figures are distances in typical 30-minute moves. The divisor is the measured pace of <b>the session the clock is in</b>, not an all-hours average: RTY does not move at one speed, and a median that blends a dead overnight hour with the New York open describes neither of them. Right now that is <b>European hours</b>, where one move is <b>3.9 pt</b> (median of 1465 clean half-hour windows in the 680-hour archive). At the bell that median becomes <b>5.8 pt</b>, so every × here divides by about <b>1.5×</b> when the cash session opens. The distance does not change; the reach does. Under <b>1×</b> is inside normal reach for the next half hour; past <b>2×</b> needs a fresh catalyst or a change of session.</div></div>
