<!-- SCENARIOS -->
<section class="card" id="rail-scenario-branch-map">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Scenario branch map</div>
    <!-- Rendered by renderScenarios from SCENARIOS itself. The line that
         used to be here said "branch C is anchored to the market-implied
         July hike odds (~30%)" and went on saying it after branch C had
         been rewritten into something with no connection to hike odds. -->
    <div class="card-sub" id="scenSub"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>3 branches, summing to <b>100%</b>. The modal case is <b>A · The break was a flush and the shelf is reclaimed</b> at 45%. </summary><div class="fwork-b">Every branch carries the level that invalidates it; tap one for its targets and for that level. <span class="dim">Prices in these branches are marked <b>as built</b>: they are the levels as they stood when this page was built, which is what the branches were written against — structural anchors, not live quotes. This card renders once and does not repaint, so it cannot follow the tape. The live price is on the ladder above.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body" id="scenarios"><div class="scen"><button class="scen-head" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><div><div class="scen-name">A · The break was a flush and the shelf is reclaimed</div><div class="scen-tag">The condition this branch wrote for itself has FIRED: ES closed the cash session inside its shelf — the 19:55Z auction bar, seventy-eight thousand contracts, closed 7,778.50 — the full cash-session close this branch said would make it live again. RTY came within two points of joining it and did not.</div><div class="meter"><div style="width:45%;background:var(--good)"></div></div></div><div class="scen-prob">45%<span class="scen-chev">▸</span></div></button><div class="scen-body"><div class="scen-why">Each contract still has a dense shelf, a hollow directly beneath it, and the balance this board was describing two days ago beneath the hollow; that part is measured rather than argued. ES reclaimed its shelf three times today — the payrolls half-hour, then again after a midday fade pressed the floor from beneath, and then for keeps: the cash session CLOSED at 7,778.50, inside 7,760.00 to 7,785.00, after trading 7,786.75 through the shelf’s top. It is 7,717.00 <span class="dim">as built</span> in the evening stub. RTY got to 3,041.00 on Thursday, 3,040.00 at the print, and 3,046.80 in the afternoon — its first trade INSIDE the lost shelf — but closed 3,040.90, two points under the 3,043.00 floor: its half of the reclaim printed intraday and did not close. A reclaim that printed and failed on both contracts was why this branch was cut to 30; the payrolls bid took it back to 40; the cash close its own text demanded is why it carries 45 tonight — with the two points RTY still owes named as the reason it is not higher. What still argues for it is the one thing that has not stopped: the shelf gets HEAVIER while it breaks. 26.70% of the window traded at or above 3,043.00, against the 11.06 per cent the same measurement carried yesterday, because the selling kept leaving volume behind in it on the way down. Inventory changing hands is not inventory leaving — and today the hands that took it showed up: the afternoon traded INTO the shelf on both contracts, and on ES it closed there.</div><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>First reaction &amp; targets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RTY</td><td>The shelf runs <b>3,043.00</b> to <b>3,050.00</b>, eight bins, and <b>3,046.00</b> is the heaviest of them at 1.23%, though no longer the cash-session point of control — the payrolls volume moved that crown down to the <b>3,035.00</b> bin. Spot at 3,007.40 <span class="dim">as built</span> finished the cash session two points of travel from the floor it lost — it was thirty-four points away at the European low, thirteen after the payrolls half-hour, and 3,046.80 printed INSIDE the shelf in the afternoon before the close backed off to just beneath it. The structure had never had to defend itself, then it did and it lost, and the walk back this row was waiting for has now been attempted and failed — RTY traded 3,041.00 at 22:20 HKT, two points short of 3,043.00, and gave the whole move back inside two hours. A failed reclaim is worse for this branch than no reclaim at all, because it prices who was willing to buy it. That was Thursday’s verdict; the payrolls print has forced a second vote — a 95th-percentile half-hour carried RTY from the day’s low region to 3,040.00, one point shy of Thursday’s failed-reclaim high, and it now trades from the middle of the ladder rather than staring up at it from the floor. The afternoon held the third vote: 3,046.80 traded, THROUGH the floor and inside the shelf, before the close settled two points back beneath it — each attempt has ended higher than the last, which is the shape of a level being worn down rather than defended. The ladder this branch had to climb was climbed on Friday and eaten on the way up, and the weekend has put one rung back — on paper rather than on the tape, which is a distinction worth the sentence. WITHDRAWN — this row said <b>3,030.44</b> was still absent and that nothing had re-formed there. Both halves were false the day they were written, and what has happened since is the more useful fact: the pool that re-formed keys <b>3,029.48</b>, carries sixty-nine turns and was last touched one hundred and sixty-four hours ago, and it has drifted to the far edge of the tolerance that lets this page call two prices one object. A little further and they stop being one object — not because anything traded there, but because a cluster boundary moved under a level nobody has been near in days. Emptied, rebuilt and then quietly re-keyed is a different history from emptied and left empty, and the last of those three steps is the one no chart will show you. The rung above it came back on the map overnight, and this line then said it had gone again. That claim is WITHDRAWN rather than restated: what stands in that band now is 3,038.54, and an absence cannot be asserted at a price a pool occupies. The level was listed, de-listed and listed again inside three sessions before this row stopped watching it, which is the behaviour worth carrying forward — not any one of those states. Twice in three sessions this level has been listed and de-listed without the market doing anything there, which is worth more as a warning about the measurement than as news about the level — a cluster boundary a few tenths wide will hand you a pool and take it away again on the same tape. Overhead is NOT unchanged, and that is the finding. The rung that stood at the top of the shelf is no longer on the map at all — it was not taken, it dissolved as Monday and Tuesday re-formed the clustering beneath it. <b>3,046.45</b> is WITHDRAWN — nothing is clustered there now — and so is 3,036.52. THE CONSOLIDATION IS THE FINDING, and it is larger than either withdrawal. Three shelves this row described separately inside a ten-point band have become one object over eleven days: <b>3,038.54</b> now holds eighty-six turns, thirty-three of them cash-made, last touched one hundred and sixty-five hours ago. It is not one of the three relocated. At 0.23, 2.02 and 7.91 bins from the prices they were keyed to, no single predecessor is near enough to be the same structure under a new key; what happened is that a band this row read as three separate rungs traded into one shelf, and the count that matters is the new object’s, not the sum of theirs. <b>3,002.00</b> is the other anchor and it is still the low of the trailing day, thirty-nine points beneath the close — the standing start at the bottom of the day was converted into the top of the ladder inside one session. Note what kept moving, because it finally moved the other way: 3,015.00 was the low when this row was first written and price made a lower one three times; today it made a HIGHER high three times through the payrolls session and the afternoon.</td></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>The same structure at a different scale, and the day ended it split finer than any session yet: ES closed the cash session inside its shelf; RTY traded inside its own — 3,046.80 against a 3,043.00 floor — and could not close there. The convergence of damage this row opened with is gone; what is left is a two-point question on the smaller contract. <b>7,760.00</b> to <b>7,785.00</b> is eleven bins, 20.92% of the window between them, and still the densest stretch on the ES map. What it carries has now resolved three times in one day. At the print the payrolls bid consumed everything resting at <b>7,766.49</b>; the level rebuilt within the hour as price camped on it; the afternoon walk consumed it AGAIN on the way to the close — and it has REBUILT A THIRD TIME since. <b>7,764.36</b> now carries one hundred and thirteen turns — heavier than either of the rebuilds described above, and the heaviest this level has held all week. That is the SIXTH state of one level: emptied, defended-and-losing, consumed, rebuilt, consumed again, and now rebuilt heavier than before. The absence this row used to record has been overtaken; the level is back and it is thicker than it was. This row spent the week reading the level as defended-and-losing while price walked away; the day validated the defence twice over, and the second consumption is the cleaner signal: resting interest at the centre of the shelf keeps getting filled in the buyers’ direction and keeps re-forming until it is filled again. It sits under the cash point of control, which Wednesday's session moved to <b>7,772.50</b>, so the price the session keeps turning at and the price it spent its time at are the same price. What has changed is the distance. The trailing range now runs <b>7,725.50</b> to <b>7,786.75</b>; ES spent the European morning near the bottom of it, the payrolls half-hour carried it back to the top quarter, and the cash close printed 7,778.50 — the top sixth of the day, inside the shelf. The void underneath did not fill on the way up: from <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,745.00</b> there are forty-three bins, 41.30% of the window between all of them, 0.96% a bin against a 0.53% average for the contract — ES fell into that stretch on Thursday and has been lifted back out, so the hollow is once again beneath price rather than around it. <b>7,636.42</b> is the first real structure beneath the fresh pools, well over a hundred points down. The narrowing this row was watching resolved in one half-hour: the reclaim both contracts needed, ES was handed intraday — RTY’s never quite printed — and what neither has is the cash close that turns an intraday visit into a reclaim.</td></tr><tr><td>HSI</td><td>The reference close stays flagged and stays untrusted, so read the session in its own prices. Friday’s HK session is the first counter-example in five days: it opened at 25,526.65, traded to 25,669.52, and finished at 25,668.03 — parked on its own high, the first session this week HK has closed on strength inside its own hours rather than selling it. One session against four is a change of behaviour rather than a change of regime. Note the direction, because it rhymes with the rest of this branch: HK stopped selling on the same day the payrolls bid stopped the RTY slide.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="invalid">CALL: this branch was tradeable on Thursday and it lost, and that is the first thing on it. The trigger as written — long ES on a five-minute close back above 7,760.00, stop under 7,746.75 — printed at 14:00Z on the leg that carried to 7,768.50, and the stop printed at 15:15Z on a five-minute close of 7,739.25. Call it twenty-two points against, taken exactly as specified, recorded here rather than quietly re-specified afterwards. The RTY half of the same call never triggered at all: it needed a cash bar closing back above 3,043.00 and the high of the day was 3,041.00, two points short — so the contract this board told you NOT to buy is the one that cost nothing. It is worth naming why the ES trigger was a bad trigger before it was a losing one. ES closed a five-minute bar on the other side of 7,760.00 twenty-five separate times that Thursday. A level-close entry on a price oscillating through that level is not an entry, it is a coin flip with commission attached, and the repair is a distance or a persistence filter rather than a different level: a close a full point clear of it, or two consecutive closes on the same side. RE-ENTRY, because this is a re-entry board and not a management one: the payrolls print has moved this branch from ‘no long here’ to ‘restore condition live’. THE CONDITION HAS FIRED. This row demanded a full cash-session close inside 7,760.00 to 7,785.00 — not a five-minute close, not a payrolls spike — and the bell delivered exactly that: the closing auction printed 7,778.50, eighteen points inside the floor, after the fade this row feared pressed 7,760.00 from beneath at midday and was bought back. The slower timeframe was chosen for precisely today and it did its job in both directions: it kept this branch out of the morning whipsaw, and it certifies the reclaim now. What the weekend holds: the branch is live by its own written test; RTY’s half remains unclosed (3,046.80 traded, 3,040.90 closed, floor at 3,043.00); and Monday opens on a gap this page cannot see past. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT from here: a Monday open that trades back under 7,760.00 and stays there through the first hour — a reclaim that fails over a weekend is Thursday’s lesson at a larger scale. Two calibration notes that still apply. In cash hours the reach model UNDER-states ES travel by 25%, so ES levels arrive sooner than the arithmetic says once the bell rings; on RTY it OVER-states by 18% in cash hours, so RTY levels are further away than the board reads. And the archive behind this window is a SIX-MINUTE SAMPLE rather than a tape: it can establish that a level was crossed and can never establish that one was untouched.</div></div></div><div class="scen"><button class="scen-head" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><div><div class="scen-name">B · The break continues and the hollow does its work</div><div class="scen-tag">Both contracts confirmed it on Thursday; the payrolls print bought half of it back and the cash close bought most of the rest, which is why this branch ends the week second on the board.</div><div class="meter"><div style="width:30%;background:var(--warning)"></div></div></div><div class="scen-prob">30%<span class="scen-chev">▸</span></div></button><div class="scen-body"><div class="scen-why">RTY did not fall THROUGH the hollow on Thursday, it filled the hollow and then fell out of the bottom of it: between 3,019.00 and 3,042.00 there are twenty-four bins, and the 7.04% of the window they held when this branch was written has become 28.81% — 1.20% a bin against a 0.65% average for the contract, so the stretch price was supposed to drop through ended up DENSER than the map around it. That read like a defeat for this branch and was scored the opposite while spot sat beneath the stretch; the payrolls half-hour has flipped the side that mattered. Spot is 3,007.40 <span class="dim">as built</span>, back INSIDE the stretch, trading through inventory this branch needs to act as a ceiling. ES un-confirmed at the same moment and then buried the case at the bell: it CLOSED the cash session inside its shelf, at 7,778.50. This branch needed the print’s move to fail — a 30-minute close back under 3,015.00 — and got the opposite: RTY closed twenty-six points above that line after trading inside its own shelf. What this branch keeps is the record, and one live fact: the RTY close still sits under 3,043.00, so the break has been un-broken everywhere except the one line that started it.</div><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>First reaction &amp; targets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RTY</td><td>Price WAS out of the hollow, out of the BOTTOM of it, for exactly one overnight session — the sentence this row waited three refreshes to write, un-written by the payrolls print inside half an hour. Between <b>3,019.00</b> and <b>3,042.00</b> there are twenty-four bins, and the 7.04% of the window they held when this row was written has become 28.81% — 1.20% a bin against a 0.65% average for the contract, so the twenty-three points price was supposed to fall through ended up thicker than the map around them. The heaviest of them is <b>3,035.00</b> at 1.60%, and every bit of that weight was laid down by the session that was meant to be falling through it. What matters is which side of it price stands on, and the payrolls half-hour changed the answer. Spot at 3,007.40 <span class="dim">as built</span> is no longer beneath the stretch, it is back inside it, trading through the inventory this row read as a ceiling — and two full sessions have since been dealt inside it, which has rebuilt this part of the map rather than confirmed the old reading of it. Monday sold RTY 10.90 points while ES held its bid; Tuesday inverted that exactly, RTY taking 14.50 back while ES gave up 15.50 — opposite directions on consecutive days, both of them transacted here. What they left is three clusters where this row saw inventory it expected to be crossed once: <b>3,029.48</b> and <b>3,022.08</b>, plus a third that is now <b>3,036.52</b> — WITHDRAWN, with nothing clustered at that price any more. The two that stand carry sixty-nine and seventy-six turns respectively. That last one has been taken into 3,038.54, eighty-six turns, which formed across the band above it. So this part of the map is no longer three objects rather than one: the upper two of the three have become the one. <b>3,030.44</b> was run clean out of the map by the payrolls bid, and it has since rebuilt — the first of the three clusters above IS that level re-formed, keyed <b>3,029.48</b> and carrying sixty-nine turns. This row asserted it was gone for two days while listing it three sentences earlier. The rung above briefly re-appeared under a new key overnight and is now unlisted again, its turns having been Friday’s all along — a boundary redrawn twice, not a level rebuilt. What this row was watching as ceiling was traded through on Friday, in the direction that argues against the branch writing it, and nothing since has tested that verdict. The day’s high at <b>3,041.00</b> was the top of Thursday’s failed attempt, and the payrolls push has now printed 3,040.00, one point shy of it — twice rejected two-and-change under the shelf floor is the strongest fact left on this row’s side. <b>3,002.00</b> is the trailing day’s low, thirty-nine points beneath the close: the fresh session opened in the bottom of the old one and the payrolls day lifted it all the way out. Underneath is where the map has gone thin, which is the other half of the change. The four bins from <b>3,015.00</b> to <b>3,018.00</b> are 4.60% of the window between all of them, 1.15% a bin — thicker than the map’s 0.65% average, and spot has been carried back above them — the thin stretch beneath is underfoot again rather than the air price was falling through. The thin part starts under 3,015.00 and runs down. Widen it and the picture holds: <b>2,988.00</b> to <b>3,015.00</b> is twenty-eight bins, 21.56% of the window between all of them, 0.77% a bin. <b>3,005.00</b> at 1.04% is the only thing resembling a shelf in the first twelve points down. Then the shelf Monday’s cash session built, where it stops being thin: <b>2,988.00</b> is the densest bin in that stretch. The pool that anchored that stretch is no longer on the map — three sessions of trade above it re-formed the clustering, and the nearest thing beneath price now is <b>3,004.26</b> at seventy-eight turns. The <b>2,951.11 pool</b> has sixteen turns, eight of them Asian, on a round number, and it is the oldest structure on this part of the map by a wide margin. Then <b>2,941.00</b>, and <b>2,936.00</b> — the old balance’s heaviest bin, though no longer the point of control across all hours: one payrolls day moved that crown ninety-nine points up the map, to <b>3,035.00</b>.</td></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>The trigger printed a third time and held into Thursday’s close; the payrolls print has taken ES back above it, which un-does the difference this row was rewritten to record. The void under the shelf runs from <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,757.50</b>: forty-eight bins, 46.87% of the window between all of them, up from 13.67% when this row was written. Most of that thickening is still at the ceiling — the five bins from <b>7,747.50</b> up come to 5.57% between them, and <b>7,752.50</b> alone carries 1.31% — but spot has been carried back OVER that ceiling — the 13:00Z thirty-minute bar closed at 7,770.25, thirteen points above it. Take those five off and the body, <b>7,640.00</b> to <b>7,745.00</b>, forty-three bins, comes to 41.30% of the window between all of them, 0.96% a bin against a 0.53% average for the contract. ES fell inside that on Thursday and has been lifted back out. A hundred points of thin, and spot spent the European morning in it — a sentence this row got to keep for one session before the print un-wrote it. What it is doing in there deserves more attention than the fact that it is there. <b>7,735.00</b> at 1.51% is the heaviest bin in the entire body of the void, with <b>7,732.50</b> at 1.45% just beneath it, and neither was there on Thursday morning, so Thursday’s session filled the void from the top downward as it walked. That slow work was the shape of a void being worked rather than gapped — and it is why the payrolls reversal weighs so much here: levels built at walking pace are real, and they traded back through in one half-hour anyway. What it lands on is the densest thing ES has built all week. <b>7,632.50</b> at 0.49% is the heaviest bin down there, with <b>7,630.00</b> just under it at 0.49%, and the <b>7,636.42 pool</b> sits on top of them with twenty-four turns, twelve of them Asian and ten European, and it is the oldest structure in the cluster. That cluster is real support and it is well over a hundred points beneath the market. Beneath it <b>7,552.39</b> is a bin and NOT a pool — no resting turns are recorded there, only volume traded through it — and <b>7,470.00</b> is the floor of the balance the market left behind in July.</td></tr><tr><td>HSI</td><td>HK’s Friday session came and went without the US delivering anything to gap into — and then closed on its own high. Whatever this branch does next resolves in US hours and lands on Monday’s HK open; carrying it over the weekend gap is a bet on Monday’s US tape wearing an HSI ticker, the same trade with worse liquidity and a prior close this page has flagged.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="invalid">CALL: both triggers have now printed and the trade as written has worked, which is a thing this board should say as plainly when it happens as when it does not. On RTY the five-minute close under 3,043.00 came at Thursday’s cash open. The sell zone this row specified — a failure in 3,038.00 to 3,043.00, stop above 3,043.00 — filled at 22:10 HKT when the reclaim ran to 3,041.00 and turned two points shy of the shelf’s floor, and the stop was never threatened because 3,043.00 was never traded. First target 3,021.10 filled in the last half hour of the cash session. Second target 3,005.00 has FILLED — the low is 3,002.00, three points through it. On ES the restated trigger — a five-minute close below 7,746.75 — printed at 15:15Z at 7,739.25 and held through Thursday’s close, giving none of it back inside that session. MANAGEMENT, for once, rather than re-entry: the first target is banked, so the runner is free. Stop to entry on the balance, and the level that matters for the rest of it is <b>3,015.00</b>, the floor of the four bins price has just dropped out of. While price stays under it the thin part of the map is doing the work; a reclaim of it puts price back inside a hollow that is now DENSER than the map around it, which is a different trade and not this one. UPDATE AT THE PRINT: that reclaim HAS fired — the 13:00Z thirty-minute bar closed at 3,032.40, seventeen points back inside the hollow — so by this row’s own sentence the runner’s edge is gone. The payrolls session printed 3,040.00 into the 3,038.00-to-3,043.00 entry zone, so the balance is in all likelihood flat at entry under the stop-to-entry rule — the exact fill decides that, not this page — and the afternoon settled direction by closing the cash session at 3,040.90, inside the zone itself. What the campaign banked before the print: first target in full, second target in full, and at worst a scratch on the runner. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT: RTY trading back up into 3,043.00 to 3,050.00 and leaving on the upside within the hour, which converts a shelf that broke into a shelf that was defended and is the strongest bullish signal available on this board. UPDATE AT THE CLOSE: the first half of that has happened — RTY traded 3,046.80, inside the zone — and the second half has not: it backed out to close 3,040.90, beneath the floor. Entered-and-rejected is not the signal; it is the zone doing its job as supply, and this branch survives the weekend on that two-point rejection. Short of that, the pace hazard this row named has resolved against the branch: the void ES was working one bin at a time gave back the whole walk in a single payrolls half-hour. The scheduled-headline warning written here belonged to an earlier session’s clock and retires with the position; the rule it stated survives it — a thin book plus a scheduled print is how stops become suggestions — and the next such print belongs to the next brief’s calendar, not to this card.</div></div></div><div class="scen"><button class="scen-head" type="button" aria-expanded="false"><div><div class="scen-name">C · It goes back up and retakes the highs</div><div class="scen-tag">A forecast on Tuesday, half-run on Wednesday, failed into Thursday — then handed a 95th-percentile payrolls print and a cash close that did most of the repair work, which is the whole of the news.</div><div class="meter"><div style="width:25%;background:var(--warning)"></div></div></div><div class="scen-prob">25%<span class="scen-chev">▸</span></div></button><div class="scen-body"><div class="scen-why">RTY has now traded <b>3,046.80</b>, inside the shelf it lost, eleven under the <b>3,058.00</b> top of its window — and closed two points beneath the shelf floor at <b>3,043.00</b>. ES traded <b>7,786.75</b>, through its shelf’s top and thirty-three under its own <b>7,820.00</b>, and closed the cash session at 7,778.50. Neither contract has reached the highs this branch is named after, but both finished the week pointed at them rather than away from them, which no session since Tuesday could say. What kept this branch alive was what stood above and who built it, and half of that has been withdrawn by the tape rather than by me: the shelf-edge pool is off the map. What this row named above spot on RTY, <b>3,046.45</b>, is WITHDRAWN — nothing is clustered there now, and the weight that was in that band sits in 3,038.54 instead; the ES structure this row compared against, <b>7,820.25</b>, is WITHDRAWN — nothing is clustered there now — it has left the map, so there is no age left to quote and the comparison has lost one of its two sides. Where a week ago the only structure above was overnight inventory. A high the bell built is a high the bell has to defend or hand back.</div><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>First reaction &amp; targets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RTY</td><td>Above <b>3,049.00</b> the map still runs out, and today did not change that. 19.60% of the window traded at or above 3,049.00 — thirty-one bins out of one hundred and seventy-five. Seven points higher it collapses: 12.81% of the window traded at or above 3,056.00 — twenty-four bins out of one hundred and seventy-five. The high itself is <b>3,058.00</b>, one bin holding 0.91% of the window, the top of the whole measurement and the thinnest print RTY has made in six days. That is a spike rather than an auction: price went up, found nobody, and left. What was up there is no longer up there. That pool has left the map without being traded through — RTY’s high across Monday and Tuesday was 3,045.80, so price never reached it and it was not consumed; the cluster simply re-formed lower and the key went with it. The object this row called the one above spot, <b>3,046.45</b>, is WITHDRAWN — nothing rests at that price now, so it has no turns, no age and no rank left to quote. What occupies the band is 3,038.54 at eighty-six turns, first-heaviest of the RTY stop map by score. Everything scoring at or above it sits BELOW it in price, and Friday’s climb took two of them: <b>3,030.44</b> was run straight through by the payrolls bid and has since been re-posted: it keys <b>3,029.48</b> now, carrying sixty-nine turns. A level the map stops listing is showing you consumption, the one exit that kind of structure is built for, and Friday printed it twice on the way up — but consumption is not removal, and this one came back inside three sessions. One of the two flickered back onto the map under a shifted key overnight and has since dropped off it again, carrying turns that predated the reopen throughout; the honest reading is a clustering boundary moving twice, not stops being re-posted over a weekend. That is the finding — the ladder under the shelf was eaten by exactly the move this branch needed. It also sits on the upper edge of the <b>3,043.00</b> to <b>3,050.00</b> shelf RTY lost on Thursday, so this branch has to travel the whole shelf before it gets there: 26.70% of the window traded at or above 3,043.00 — thirty-seven bins out of one hundred and seventy-five — and price is underneath all of it. The measurement beneath has swung all the way to this branch’s side in one day. WITHDRAWN — the all-hours value area no longer tops out at <b>3,060.00</b>, which it did when this was written, at the window high then — that ceiling stood at 3,020 this morning — and the busiest price across all hours is now <b>3,035.00</b>. The old balance at 2,936 no longer holds that crown; one payrolls day moved it ninety-nine points up the map. Value did not creep toward the highs, it relocated to them in a single session. Spot at 3,007.40 <span class="dim">as built</span> finished the cash session inside the new value’s upper half. What is left of the bear case here is one line: the shelf floor at 3,043.00, tagged to 3,046.80 and not closed above.</td></tr><tr><td>ES</td><td>The same region, a completely different answer, and this is the divergence that matters. 13.19% of the window traded at or above 7,787.50 — twenty-one bins out of one hundred and ninety-six — where RTY’s equivalent stretch is nearly empty. 5.57% of the window traded at or above 7,810.00, twelve bins out of one hundred and ninety-six. Where RTY has a rounding error near its high, ES has an auction. The strongest single statement on this board about the ES upside is a boundary rather than a level. <b>7,820.00</b> is no longer the top of the window nor, measured across all hours, the upper edge of the 70% value area — so value expanded all the way to the high instead of being left behind by it. RTY could not say that this morning — its value area then topped out thirty-eight points beneath its window high — and by the close it nearly could: one payrolls session expanded RTY’s value to within a point of its own high, so the divergence this row was built on has closed. What remains of the old balance is a bin rather than a pool, down there — the turns that made it are still in the profile, but no cluster keys on them any more. The pool this row quoted here yesterday is no longer on the map at all, which is its own small piece of evidence: the structure down there is thinning rather than building. WITHDRAWN — yesterday this row said the ES map carried no inferred stop of any kind above 7,782.11. That is no longer true. WITHDRAWN — <b>7,820.25</b> is no longer a cluster of any kind: the two cash-session turns printed on the exact top of the measurement have left the map, and the all-hours 70% band no longer reaches up to it. Two turns was thin evidence and a two-turn cluster is the easiest kind to run; the point it was making — that something was now there where nothing had been — is withdrawn, because that something has since left the map. What changed today is the distance to it, twice. The payrolls half-hour closed most of it, the afternoon closed more, and the cash session ended at 7,778.50 with spot at 7,717.00 <span class="dim">as built</span> in the evening stub — the top of the map is inside a normal session’s reach for the first time since this branch was written. The structure between here and there turned over completely on the way and has since rebuilt: <b>7,764.36</b> was consumed at the print, rebuilt within the hour, consumed a SECOND time by the afternoon walk, and now carries one hundred and thirteen turns again — while the day’s buying left two heavyweights bracketing the close, and the reopen has thickened the upper one. Well beneath, <b>7,755.66</b> no longer carries a pool at all — the structure that stood there has left the map, and it is NOT re-pointed at the nearest survivor: that one is four bins away and is the very level this paragraph has just been discussing, so moving the sentence onto it would make one level stand in for two. OVERTAKEN — the structure the Asian session built here was withdrawn when it left the map, and the reasoning for withdrawing it rather than re-anchoring was sound WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN: the only candidate then sat 1.25 bins away on a different turn count. It has since been overtaken by events. <b>7,784.24</b> is where that cluster keys now, on one hundred and four turns. When this row called it a re-formation rather than a neighbour it stood just under half a bin from the price the withdrawal named; it has widened since, and the reading that justified the call is no longer the reading the map holds. Heavy enough is still true and is not the half spending itself. Close enough is, and when it runs out the page will stop being able to call the two one object — a boundary moving rather than a level going anywhere, which is the fact worth carrying — Friday’s closing-auction cluster at the shelf top has been consumed on the way through. On <b>7,820.25</b>, nothing clusters any more: the turns printed at the top have left the map, and the claim is withdrawn rather than moved to a neighbour.</td></tr><tr><td>HSI</td><td>Has the least to say about this branch and should not be asked. Nothing here resolves in HK hours.</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="invalid">CALL: no chase, and on RTY no entry at all until branch A has happened first. RTY is beneath the <b>3,043.00</b> shelf it lost at the cash open, so a long here is not a breakout trade, it is a reclaim trade wearing a breakout’s clothes, and the two want different stops. The sequence this branch NEEDED on RTY was a cash close back above 3,043.00, then the structure above that line taken and held through a full cash hour on real volume. THE SECOND LEG CANNOT BE SATISFIED AS WRITTEN: that pool is gone from the map, and it went without being traded through, so the condition has been voided by a structure change rather than resolved by the market. It is left as written rather than re-pointed at a nearer price — quietly moving a trigger onto a different object is how a condition stops meaning what it was agreed to mean. The restatement this row offered is no longer available: it would have pointed the second leg at <b>3,046.45</b>, which is WITHDRAWN and holds nothing to point at. It is NOT re-pointed at 3,038.54, the object that consumed that band — moving a leg onto a structure that formed after the branch was written is the same quiet substitution this paragraph already refuses one sentence above. Only then is the stop under 3,043.00 — the same line as branch A, because a breakout that needs a different stop from the pullback it replaces is two trades pretending to be one. ES is the cleaner expression and always was on this branch, though it is now under its shelf rather than on it, which puts the trigger further away rather than making it different: a cash close above <b>7,787.50</b>, forty-eight points overhead from here. The stop moves DOWN rather than up, and the reason is a correction. Yesterday this row put it at 7,746.75 on the argument that the line had been closed through twice and reclaimed twice, so price argued about it rather than respecting it. Today it was closed through at 15:15Z and NOT reclaimed — price has left it, which makes it a poor stop and a worse story. A long that needs a reclaim of 7,787.50 to be right has no business being stopped out above the day’s low, so the stop belongs under the session low at <b>7,724.25</b>: wider, fewer contracts, same money at risk. WHAT WOULD BREAK IT: ES back at its own high while RTY is still under 3,043.00. That is the non-confirmation, and note that it has swapped ends since yesterday — the worry used to be the small cap making a high the broad index could not follow, and today it is the broad index leaving the small cap under a level it already lost. Read it as a warning about the ES long rather than as a reason to buy RTY cheaply. Two measurement caveats belong on any branch that quotes an absence. Every unmapped claim above is a claim about COVERAGE, because the archive samples once every six minutes and can never prove a level was untouched. And that archive begins on 30 July with a thirteen-and-a-half-hour hole in it, plus a second unrecoverable hole of 9.83 hours across Tuesday’s Asian and European sessions from the squash.</div></div></div></div>
</section>

<!-- CHAIN -->
<section class="card" id="rail-cross-market-chain">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Cross-market chain</div>
    <div class="card-sub">Which links are still transmitting and which have gone dead. Rewritten twice this week — tap a link for why.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body"><div class="chain" id="chain"><div class="node"><div class="node-name">The long end</div><div class="node-val">10-yr 4.69% on the feed · 30-yr 5.22% — and it IS on this feed now (treasury.gov daily), so the 5.15% regime line is testable from this page: it sits exactly on the 2004–2007 monthly plateau (5.15–5.20 at the tops of Aug 04, Jun 06, Jun 07). The last calendar month whose AVERAGE 30-yr sat at or above today’s 5.22 was <b>July 2004</b> — twenty-two years — measured 7 Aug against the full monthly series since 1977, so “multidecade” is now a dated fact rather than a headline word · Tuesday cash: RUT +1.850%, NDX beat it by 145bp</div><div class="chain-bi"><div class="fbreak"><span class="fbi" style="color:var(--warning)">Armed</span><b>the long-end DEFEAT — RUT beating NDX by more than 50bp on a session the long end moved 2bp or less (re-specified 13 Aug)</b> — RUT over NDX -22.0bp is 72.0 from the 50.0bp that breaks it; the 10-year’s move 2.0bp is at or below 2.0bp; the 30-year’s move 1.0bp is at or below 2.0bp (session 2026-08-26)</div></div></div><div class="link weakened"><button class="link-btn" type="button"><span style="color:var(--warning)">◆</span> <span>→ RTY / small caps — TWICE NOW — a second day of rate relief and the Russell still did not lead</span></button><div class="link-why">The rule rewritten on 28 Jul after it cost money says small caps cannot lead while the long end is under pressure. It has now been handed two clean relief days in a row and it has survived both, and the way it survived them is the part worth being uncomfortable about. Monday: the curve fell five to six basis points at every tenor on the Hormuz de-escalation headline, 2s10s FLATTER rather than steeper, and RUT closed +1.72% against NDX +1.77% — a dead heat on the exact input this chain has said for a month is the only configuration that lets the Russell lead. Tuesday: another 5.9bp off the ten-year, 4.686% to 4.627%, and RUT closed +1.850%, which is a strong day in isolation and a loss of 145bp to the Nasdaq in the only comparison this node makes. Twice given what it asked for, twice no leadership. The stated break condition — the thirty-year closing below 5.15% — is not testable from this page today, because the thirty-year is not on this feed and the last reading held here is Monday’s 5.23%. That figure is carried, not current, and it is not restated as though it were. Now the defect, which is in the node rather than in the market. The DEFEAT condition written on 3 Aug is RUT out-gaining NDX by more than 50bp on a day the long end is UNCHANGED — leadership without permission from rates. The ten-year has moved on every session since it was written. A condition that requires a flat tape in a week that has not produced one cannot fire, and a condition that cannot fire is indistinguishable from a condition that keeps passing, which is the failure this whole board is built against. It is left exactly as written. The alternative is to loosen ‘unchanged’ until the week can satisfy it, and refitting a rail so a pinned component moves again is how a measurement turns into an opinion — the oil node below refuses that same trade and this one does not get an exemption for being inconvenient. What it needs is a flat-rates session, and if the week ends without one the condition should be re-specified deliberately and dated, not quietly widened. RE-SPECIFIED 13 AUG, and the paragraph above is left standing because it is the reason this was not done sooner. The week ended without a flat-rates session, which is the trigger that paragraph wrote for itself, so the condition is re-cut here rather than quietly widened — and the threshold is DERIVED rather than chosen. Across the ten completed US sessions the relay has banked (30 July to 12 August, which is a thin sample and is stated as one), the absolute day-over-day move in the long end sorts to 0, 0, 2, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8 basis points on the ten-year and 0, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5, 5, 6, 7 on the thirty. The bottom quartile of that pooled distribution is TWO BASIS POINTS at both the 25th and the 33rd percentile, so “the long end did not move” is now two basis points or less on BOTH tenors, measured on treasury.gov closes. Four of those ten sessions qualify, so the condition can fire, which the old one could not. THE NUMBER WAS TAKEN FROM THE DISTRIBUTION AND NOT FROM THE ANSWER, and the reader should be told that the answer is sensitive to it: at one basis point only two sessions qualify and Tuesday 11 August is not one of them; at two, it is. One basis point is roughly the tenth percentile, not the bottom quartile, so it would have to be argued for rather than derived. WHAT THE RE-SPECIFIED CONDITION SAYS ABOUT TUESDAY. On 11 August the ten-year moved two basis points and the thirty-year one, both inside the threshold, and RUT beat NDX by 66 basis points. The DEFEAT fires. The rule that small caps cannot lead while the long end is under pressure has been beaten once, on a session where rates gave no permission either way. THE READING THAT DISAGREES, stated rather than buried, because two honest readings of one sentence is exactly the case this board says to disclose. “Unchanged” can be read as a magnitude — the long end did not move — or as a direction: the long end did not EASE, since the rule has always been about small caps needing relief. Under the direction reading Tuesday is excluded, because both tenors fell, and the node survives. The magnitude reading is adopted, for a reason that is about the rule rather than about the outcome: the node’s own examples of “clean relief days” were five and six basis point moves, so a two basis point drift is inside the noise the rule was never about — and the direction reading admits one session in ten, which puts the condition straight back where it started, unable to fire. ONE DEFEAT IS NOT A RETIREMENT. It is one session, recorded with its terms, on a rule that has been handed relief twice and refused to lead both times. What it removes is the position this node was in for a week: a claim nothing could contradict.</div></div><div class="node"><div class="node-name">Megacap concentration</div><div class="node-val">Tuesday cash: NDX beat SPX by 152bp · RUT +1.850% — the dispersion came back and the re-arm still did not fire</div></div><div class="link broken"><button class="link-btn" type="button"><span style="color:var(--critical)">✕</span> <span>→ Index level vs breadth — STILL RETIRED — one leg of the re-arm arrived, the other did not, which is why it was written with two</span></button><div class="link-why">This node was retired on 3 Aug because Friday did not fit it, and the re-arm condition was written down at the same moment: an up day in which NDX out-gains SPX by more than 50bp WHILE RUT is negative. Tuesday delivered the first leg emphatically and the second not at all. NDX beat SPX by 152bp, three times the threshold and the widest dispersion since Friday. RUT closed +1.850%, one of its better sessions of the week. So the tape produced concentration in the growth complex without producing the breadth failure that would make concentration the story, and a node re-armed on the first leg alone would now be back on the board describing a day the small-cap index rose 1.85%. That is the entire argument for writing re-arm conditions with two legs and for not editing one when the other arrives. Dispersion is not direction: everything went up, and the Nasdaq went up more. The live single-name evidence cuts the same way and is worth more than the dispersion figure. AMD reported Tuesday after the close and beat and raised — revenue $11.54B against roughly $11.25B to $11.31B expected, adjusted EPS $1.66 against $1.60 to $1.62, data-centre revenue doubled, and Q3 guided to about $13.0B plus or minus $300M against a $12.51B consensus — and fell six and a half to seven per cent in the after-hours session, having come into the print up 142.2% on the year. Palantir reported Monday, beat on every line, guided Q3 to $2.16B, and rose about 10% toward $140. A beat-and-raise sold and a beat-and-raise bought in the same forty-eight hours is a statement about positioning rather than about fundamentals, and the one that was sold was the one that had already tripled. This board should be explicit that it holds no view on AMD. What it holds is the mechanism: if the growth complex is priced past its own delivery, the NDX-over-RUT ordering can inflect without the long end doing anything at all — which is precisely the test the node above cannot get a flat-rates day to run. Re-arm is unchanged: NDX over SPX by more than 50bp on an up day with RUT negative.</div></div><div class="node"><div class="node-name">Dollar and gold</div><div class="node-val">gold 4,229.40, +3.27% from Tuesday’s 4,095.40 close, session LOW 4,121.60 — the whole range above the 4,119.40 trigger · DXY 99.803, session high 99.90, never saw 100</div></div><div class="link transmitting"><button class="link-btn" type="button"><span style="color:var(--good)">✓</span> <span>→ Risk appetite — CONFIRMED — the condition was written in advance and both legs cleared it by a margin</span></button><div class="link-why">CONFIRMED, on a condition written in advance and left alone. This node fired on Thursday because the dollar fell while yields rose and gold was bid, which is an inflation risk premium being demanded rather than financial conditions easing. It lapsed on Friday when the dollar took it back above 100, and the lapse was published, because a warning that never stands down is not a warning, it is a mood. It re-armed on Monday with both legs inside a rounding error — DXY under 100 by four hundredths, the thirty-year over 5.20% by three basis points — and this board said out loud that a condition met by 0.04 and 0.03 is met and is also one ordinary hour from not being met. What was missing was the third leg, and the confirm test was stated as exactly that: gold through 4,119.40, the overnight high, while the dollar stays beneath 100. Both halves are now true and the margin is not a rounding error. Gold is 4,229.40, up 3.27% from Tuesday’s 4,095.40 close, and the relevant number is not the last print but the session LOW of 4,121.60 — the entire overnight range traded above the trigger, so this is not a level touched once on a wick. The dollar never saw 100: DXY is 99.803 against a session high of 99.90. Two things have to be said against it, and they are said here rather than after the fact. First, this board cannot see the third leg it was worrying about on Monday: the thirty-year is not on this feed, so whether yields are still elevated while the dollar falls — the actual shape that made this node mean something — is not readable today, and the ten-year falling 5.9bp is the wrong sign for the story. A confirm on two legs of a three-leg thesis is a confirm of what was written, and it is weaker than what was meant. Second, this board has not established WHY gold moved and should not furnish a reason it did not measure; a search returned rate-expectation and dollar-weakness framing and nothing that settles it. What can be stated is the mechanical read-across: a three per cent day in the metal with the dollar pinned under 100 is the market paying up for a hedge on the night before ADP and two days before payrolls, and it is not a risk-on tape wearing a different hat. What breaks it now: the dollar closing back above 100, or gold giving back the 4,119.40 line on a session close — a hundred and ten dollars of room, which is the difference between this reading and Monday’s.</div></div><div class="node"><div class="node-name">Oil</div><div class="node-val">broken since Sunday’s gap under 81 · Tuesday round-tripped 82.25 to 74.25 · re-arm rail 85.94 is eleven and a half dollars away</div></div><div class="link broken"><button class="link-btn" type="button"><span style="color:var(--critical)">✕</span> <span>→ Headline inflation floor — BROKEN, AND FURTHER FROM RE-ARMING THAN AT ANY POINT SINCE IT WAS WRITTEN</span></button><div class="link-why">This node was written against a floor near 83 and it named its own invalidation in advance: a session close under 81 takes the war premium out of the inflation argument entirely. That break came on Sunday’s reopen as a gap — 84.67 at 22:06Z, 79.55 six minutes later, through 81.51 and 81 and 80 in a single print — and the node has been broken since. The scored MISS on the Friday call stays exactly as it was scored: a call is judged at its resolution and never re-judged when the market comes round to it over a weekend, and pretending otherwise is how a scorecard becomes fiction. What Tuesday added is a full round trip. Crude ran back to 82.25 and then gave all of it up, trading down to 74.25 — eight dollars in a session, on the Iran and Oman interim-deal headlines that Trump was trailed to speak to at 20:30Z that evening. The consequence for the chain is unchanged and now larger: with crude beneath the pre-strike floor there is no commodity floor under headline inflation this week, which makes today’s ISM services prices-paid component a cleaner read of underlying pressure than it would otherwise be. One thing this node has to disclose about its own evidence. The run to 82.25 falls inside the archive’s only unrecoverable hole — 9.83 hours from 00:25Z to 10:15Z on 4 Aug, destroyed when the droplet squashed the repository and took 4,001 commits with it. So the collapse out of that high is reconstructable from the record and the spike into it is not: the 82.25 figure is carried from the session ledger rather than from anything this page can re-derive. Re-arms above 85.94, a rail dated 30 Jul, and crude is eleven and a half dollars beneath it — which is the furthest this node has been from re-arming since it was written, and the rail is left uncut on purpose.</div></div><div class="node"><div class="node-name">Everything</div><div class="node-val">a payrolls week in which rates have eased twice and small caps have led neither time, no commodity floor under headline inflation, one node confirming on a condition it wrote in advance, and a scheduled headline into the thinnest book of the day</div></div></div></div>
</section>

<!-- SCORECARD -->
<section class="card" id="rail-call-scorecard">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Call scorecard</div>
    <div class="card-sub">Every directional call and level target I've given you, marked honestly. This is the number that tells you which parts of the framework to lean on.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div class="score-figrow" id="scoreFig"><div class="score-fig">73%</div><div class="score-figsub">clean hit rate · 30 resolved market calls<br>80% counting partials as half<br>1 more never triggered — counted in neither</div></div><div class="score-stats" id="scoreStats"><div class="sstat"><div class="sstat-v">22</div><div class="sstat-l"><i style="color:var(--good);font-style:normal">✓</i> Hit</div></div><div class="sstat"><div class="sstat-v">4</div><div class="sstat-l"><i style="color:var(--warning);font-style:normal">◐</i> Partial</div></div><div class="sstat"><div class="sstat-v">4</div><div class="sstat-l"><i style="color:var(--critical);font-style:normal">✕</i> Missed</div></div><div class="sstat"><div class="sstat-v">1</div><div class="sstat-l"><i style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-style:normal">—</i> No trade</div></div><div class="sstat"><div class="sstat-v">0</div><div class="sstat-l"><i style="color:var(--text-muted);font-style:normal">○</i> Pending</div></div></div>
    <div class="srec" id="scoreRecord"><div class="srec-track" role="img" aria-label="31 market calls, oldest to newest: 22 hit, 4 partial, 4 missed, 1 never triggered, 0 still open"><i class="spip" data-r="miss" title="20 Jul — Missed"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="21 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="21 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="partial" title="23 Jul — Partial"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="22 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="23 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="24 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="24 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="27 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="27 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="27 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="28 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="28 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="28 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="partial" title="28 Jul — Partial"></i><i class="spip" data-r="miss" title="28 Jul — Missed"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="29 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="partial" title="29 Jul — Partial"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="30 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="partial" title="31 Jul — Partial"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="31 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="miss" title="31 Jul — Missed"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="31 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="31 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="31 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="31 Jul — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="3 Aug — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="3 Aug — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="hit" title="3 Aug — Hit"></i><i class="spip" data-r="miss" title="6 Aug — Missed"></i><i class="spip" data-r="notrade" title="7 Aug — No trade"></i></div><div class="srec-cap">Oldest left; hits above the line, misses below. The most recent miss is 1 call back, on 6 Aug. The last 10 resolved ran 80% against 73% across the whole record, so the recent stretch is the stronger half — which on 10 calls is noise until it persists.</div></div><div class="card-sub" id="scoreNote" style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><details class="fwork fwork-sub"><summary>All 4 misses stay on the board permanently — a call deleted after it fails is not a record, it is advertising.</summary><div class="fwork-b">1 call specified a trigger that never fired, so no position was taken; it is in neither number above. A conditional whose condition never happened says nothing about the forecast, and counting it either way would be a claim about a trade that does not exist. The 22 build entries — a suppression, a withdrawn level, a guard added the day it caught something — sit in their own filter and are counted in neither number above. They are only ever written down after they work, so their result is hit by construction and says nothing about calibration. Folded in they read 85% instead of 73%, which is what this board reported until the two were separated.</div></details></div><div class="filters" id="scoreFilters"><button class="toggle" type="button" style="color: var(--text-primary);">All</button><button class="toggle" type="button">Hit</button><button class="toggle" type="button">Partial</button><button class="toggle" type="button">Missed</button><button class="toggle" type="button">No trade</button><button class="toggle" type="button">Pending</button><button class="toggle" type="button">Build log</button></div><div class="card-sub" id="scoreBreak" style="margin: 10px 0px 2px;"></div>
    <div id="scoreList"><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Payrolls continuation — short RTY on a 30-minute close below 3,004.90 after the 12:30Z print, first qualifying close the bar ending 13:00Z; no ES trade between 7,724.25 and 7,745.00; no MHI trade</div><div class="fdate">7 Aug</div><details class="fwork"><summary>NO TRADE, and the entry condition is the whole reason.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Targets were 2,992.00, 2,988.00 and a 2,951.11 runner, stop a 30-minute close back above 3,012.00, invalidation a full 30-minute close above 3,015.00. The judged leg was written as a precondition rather than a hope: the trade was live only if the survey internals printed soft. They did not. Payrolls came in hot enough to bid four contracts into their 84th-to-95th percentiles over the 42 minutes bracketing the print, and RTY went the other way from the first bar. THE TRIGGER NEVER FIRED, and it was not close. The first qualifying 30-minute close, the bar ending 13:00Z, printed <b>3,034.50</b> — not merely above the 3,004.90 entry but 19.5 points above the 3,015.00 invalidation, so the call was dead at the first moment it could be read. The lowest RTY traded at any point after the window opened was <b>3,023.00</b>, eighteen points above the entry, so no entry style would have filled: not a close, not a touch, not a limit sitting at the level. THE PART WORTH KEEPING is what a careless reading would have said. RTY DID trade 3,004.90 — it printed 3,002.00 at 00:20Z, twelve hours before the trade window opened, on the overnight session this call explicitly stood aside from. A status chip derived from the trailing-day range, which is what this page had until 8 Aug, would have reported the trigger level TRADED and left the reader to work out that it happened half a day early. The window-aware chip shipped that weekend reads the 30-minute closes inside the entry’s own stated hours and reported exactly what the archive reports here: invalidated at 13:00Z, never triggered. This resolution is the first live test of that machinery and it agrees with the tape. HOW TO SCORE THIS, and it is a new column rather than a judgement call. The directional read was WRONG — the tape went up hard and a looser entry would have paid for it. The entry discipline was RIGHT and cost nothing. Filing that as a hit would inflate the record with a trade nobody took; filing it as a miss would punish the one habit worth building. It is counted in neither, and the hit rate above is computed without it. The other two legs stand as written: ES was left alone between 7,724.25 and 7,745.00 and the 13:00Z close at 7,770.25 took it out of that band, which retired the no-trade by its own terms rather than converting it into an entry; MHI was left alone because Hong Kong was shut for the print, which is a decision the clock makes rather than the analyst.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--text-secondary)">—</i>No trade</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Short RTY on acceptance beneath 3,023.00 — the shelf broke, the hollow underneath it did NOT fill, and the delta profile says the whole board is standing on a shelf built on top of a void rather than on a floor</div><div class="fdate">6 Aug</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Written FLAT, as a re-entry rather than a management decision.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The evidence is a bin-for-bin difference of the two most recent profiles, which needs no value-area anchor and is not open to interpretation: every contract traded between the two builds — 535,732 ES and 52,015 RTY — landed inside twelve ES bins and eighteen RTY bins, and NOT ONE BIN DECLINED ANYWHERE. That is a shelf thickening on top of a hollow, not a hollow filling. Beneath the session floor RTY falls thirty-one bins from 3,022.00 to the first bin holding even one per cent at 2,991.00, with 8.53% of the window in the gap; ES falls forty-four bins from 7,742.50 to 7,632.50 with 12.23% in the gap. The trade is the retest of 3,024.60–3,027.30 once 3,023.00 is lost, or a sell-stop at 3,022.50 for anyone who will not sit for the retest. T1 2,999.00, T2 2,991.00, runner 2,936.00. The stop is a 30-minute close above 3,031.20 and that is deliberately not a touch. NO ES TRADE: spot sits nine points from the six-day point of control, which is the definition of no edge. NO HSI TRADE. The reaction function is the other half of the case — ADP printed 44,000 against a 68,000 forecast and ES moved 2.50 points over forty-two minutes, a 35th-percentile move; ISM services then missed by four tenths with the employment sub at 47.4 and ES fell 10.00, an 80th-percentile move. The tape ignored the jobs miss and sold the services miss, which says it is repricing the SLOPE rather than the number, and AMD beating every line and falling 8.94% after hours says the same thing from the other side of the market. RESOLVED 6 Aug, a MISS, and the way it missed is worth more than the fact that it did. The entry filled: the 30-minute bar to 13:30Z closed 3,022.60, beneath 3,023.00, and the retest zone 3,024.60–3,027.30 was traded inside the next half hour. The stop then filled too. A 30-minute close above 3,031.20 was written as the stop deliberately, to avoid being taken out on a touch, and it printed TWICE — 3,036.80 to 14:30Z and 3,031.70 to 15:00Z. Out at roughly eleven to fourteen points against. Direction was right and the trade still lost, which is the only outcome that teaches anything: price never traded 3,043.00, turned at 3,041.00, and has since made the day’s low at 3,015.00 — below the entry, on its way toward a T1 of 2,999.00 that is still eighteen points away. The defect is the stop’s LOCATION rather than its timeframe. 3,031.20 sits inside the hollow, three quarters of a point above where 3,030.44 sat before the payrolls bid emptied it — no turns are listed there now — with the 3,038.31 pool and its nine turns above that. A stop placed just over a stop pool, inside the one stretch of the map where price has no reason to slow down, is a stop placed where the sweep goes. The rule this earns: in a low-volume pocket the stop belongs ABOVE the pools that pocket contains, not between them — here that meant 3,043.00, the shelf floor, which was never touched. Wider stop, same size, fewer contracts. That is a real cost and it is smaller than this one was.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--critical)">✕</i>Missed</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Friday's 112bp cash dispersion between NDX and RUT is a cash-session artefact — the overnight tape has RTY and ES within one basis point of each other and carries no memory of it</div><div class="fdate">3 Aug</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Written on a 17bp overnight spread — RTY +0.456% and ES +0.455% against NQ +0.629% — where Friday's cash session had run 111.5bp between NDX +0.593% and RUT −0.522%.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The resolution is worth more than the score. THE STATED TEST CANNOT BE ANSWERED AT ALL, and not only for the reason recorded below it. The break clause named RTY and NQ over the first cash half-hour; the relay banks no bars for NQ, which the ledger already said. What it could not say is that the cash proxy fails too: RUT does not print a NEW value until 13:48Z, eighteen minutes after the bell, so the 13:30, 13:36 and 13:42 samples are all repeats of a price fixed before the open. Measuring the half-hour off that frozen baseline is exactly how I first got RUT +69.6bp, and the archive reader now REFUSES that window instead of answering it. What can honestly be measured, 13:48Z to the 20:00Z close, is NDX +138.4bp against RUT +148.3bp — 9.9bp apart against Friday's 111.5. The dispersion did not merely evaporate; it INVERTED. Friday's worst index led Monday's session. And it was real while it lasted: 52.8bp across 13:48–14:30, small caps on the right side of it this time, decaying to ten by the close. So the claim was right and right for the reason given — the overnight carried no memory of Friday — while the break clause was still built wrong. 52.8bp came within eight of a 60bp threshold that would have scored this call WRONG, on a divergence whose SIGN was the opposite of the one the threshold existed to catch. A dispersion condition needs a direction. This one had a magnitude and a pair of instruments the feed cannot see.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">ES has nothing measured overhead — 0.18% of ninety-seven hours traded at or above spot, so the first cash hour either extends on air or comes back into the distribution</div><div class="fdate">3 Aug</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Written with ES at 7,553.50 and resolved from the twelve archived five-minute bars covering 13:30–14:30Z.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The hour opened 7,546.25, ran to 7,600.00 and closed 7,597.25 — a 57.25-point range and a 51.00-point advance — on 46,749 lots in the first bar against 2,079 on the overnight 00:00Z bar. A contract with two bins out of eighty-four above it found new business up there, decisively, and the 7,537.50 re-entry was never revisited. THE INVALIDATION WAS THE DEFECT, NOT THE CALL. It was written as ES holding above the 7,543.50 overnight low through the whole hour, and the very first five-minute bar printed 7,542.75 — 0.75 points, three ticks, under the line — before closing 7,559.25 and never returning. Not one bar in the hour CLOSED below 7,543.50. So the condition whose job was to declare this call wrong was defeated by the opening print on precisely the tape that proved it right, and the call survives on an accident of three ticks rather than on its instrument. A stays_above anchored ON the overnight low and read against five-minute bar LOWS is taken out by the opening sweep almost every time it is written, whichever way the hour then goes, which makes it a condition carrying no information in either direction. It needs a buffer beneath the level, or it needs evaluating on closes. This is the same touch-versus-close error the board paid for on 31 July, in a new place, three days later.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Term premium, not inflation, is what is pricing this tape — and ISM prices-paid at 22:00 HKT Monday is the cleanest single test of it</div><div class="fdate">3 Aug</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Friday's ranking is the evidence and it is unusual enough to be diagnostic: SPX +0.698% BEAT NDX +0.593% while RUT fell 0.522%.</summary><div class="fwork-b">A tech story does not look like that. A rates story looks exactly like that — the broad index fine, the growth index fine, and the one index whose earnings live on the front end taking the whole hit. The 30-year closed at 5.27%, above the 5.24% that was being called a multidecade high on Thursday, and 2s10s steepened 2bp to +47bp, which is the long end doing the work rather than the short end. This breaks two ways and they are different questions. The REGIME breaks if a long-end auction stops through with the 30-year closing below 5.15% and the dollar above 100 — and it is those two numbers that are checked, because the auction is the mechanism rather than a second test: if the long end closes there, how it got there does not change the trade. The DIAGNOSIS breaks if ISM Manufacturing Prices prints at or above 73, which would hand the wheel back to inflation and make every yield move this week a price story rather than a supply one. Resolves at Friday’s payrolls close. HAND READING, 7 Aug, and it is not a resolution — the call resolves on the REGIME at tonight’s close and this settles only the branch the relay cannot read. July ISM Manufacturing Prices printed 71.1 against a prior of 73.0, so the DIAGNOSIS did NOT break, by 1.9 points. Note how close that was: the threshold WAS the prior, so an unchanged print would have broken it and the index had to fall to leave the call standing. The REGIME is going the other way and going there quickly — the 30-year was 5.17% entering Friday and is 5.22% now, seven basis points ABOVE the 5.15% that would break it, while the dollar at 99.95 is five cents BELOW the 100 that would break it. Both legs would have to move, in opposite directions, inside one session. RESOLVED 7 Aug at the payrolls close, a HIT, and the honest reading of it is narrower than the tick suggests. NEITHER break condition fired. The 30-year closed at <b>5.19%</b> — it fell three basis points on the day, travelling TOWARD the 5.15% that would have broken it and stopping four short. The dollar closed at <b>99.62</b>, having printed 99.988 intraday: twelve thousandths of a point from the 100 handle, and the closest either leg came to its own threshold all week. So one leg moved toward its break and missed, the other brushed its break and retreated. WHAT THIS HIT IS WORTH, stated because the number will be read without the sentence: this call breaks only if BOTH legs go, and a conjunctive condition is a great deal harder to break than a single one. Surviving it is weaker evidence than the hit column implies, and a board that does not say so is flattering itself by construction. The DIAGNOSIS branch was settled by hand on 7 Aug and stands as recorded — July ISM Manufacturing Prices 71.1 against a threshold of 73.0 that was also the prior, so the index had to FALL to leave the call standing, and it did. What actually happened on the day is the part worth carrying: payrolls printed hot enough to bid four contracts into their 84th-to-95th percentiles, and the long end RALLIED into it. A strong labour print that takes yields DOWN is the term-premium reading working — supply and risk premium easing, rather than growth being repriced — which is the mechanism this call named. That is corroboration, not proof: one session, and the call is judged on its stated levels rather than on this paragraph.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY at the 2,927 point of control is the decision — heaviest bin on the board, and the first real bid since 2,951</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Written at 22:10 HKT with RTY at 2,932.20 after a 47.8-point fall, and resolved from the archived five-minute bars rather than from the closing price, because the close alone would have flattered it.</summary><div class="fwork-b">No cash half-hour closed beneath the stated 2,917.94 — the lowest was 2,919.60 at the 14:00Z half-hour, 1.66 points clear. After three half-hours closing under the point of control (2,919.60, 2,924.10, 2,925.40) price reclaimed it and finished the session at 2,939.50, 34.2 points off the low. The mean-reversion case was the right one. The qualification matters more than the score: the 14:10Z low printed 2,905.30, twelve and a half points THROUGH the invalidation level. A call defined on half-hour closes survived; a stop resting at 2,917.94 was taken out an hour into the session and did not participate in the 34-point recovery. An invalidation line and a stop are not the same instrument, and this board has been writing them as though they were.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY ladder named the downside sequence: 2,957, then the 2,953 value-area high, then the 2,951.3 pool, then the 2,927 point of control</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Price walked it in order. The 13:35Z bar stopped and closed at 2,953.60 on the value-area high.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The 13:40Z bar tested 2,951.50 against the mapped 2,951.32 pool, held it, and closed back above. The 13:45Z bar broke it on 7,782 lots, the largest of the session, and ran to 2,937.50. Low so far 2,928.50, sitting on the point of control. Four mapped levels, four reactions, in the mapped order. This is what the liquidity map was built to do.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Pre-open: RTY travels further than ES on a downside open — it sat in the thinnest bin on its own profile while ES thickened beneath</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Called at 21:06 HKT, 24 minutes before the bell, with the invalidation named in advance: RTY reclaims 2,965 and holds it through the first cash half-hour.</summary><div class="fwork-b">It never did. From the opening bar's high to the session low RTY fell 1.32% against ES 0.65% — a clean 2x, and that is the number that generalises. Measured point-to-point from the call it reads 11x (RTY -0.98%, ES -0.09%), but that flatters it: ES rallied to 7,515.50 first and came back, so the endpoints hide the path. Trust the 2x, not the 11x.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">ECI: on a hawkish front-end repricing, RTY gives the overnight move back first</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Written into the catalyst card before the 12:30Z print.</summary><div class="fwork-b">On the print bar RTY fell 0.18% against ES 0.11%, on 3x prior volume against 2x, and the move extended all session — RTY -0.75% on the day with NQ still +0.46%, a 1.2-point dispersion spread. Third consecutive test of the rule rewritten on 28 Jul after it cost money. The ECI number itself was never published anywhere reachable, so only the consequent is scored; the antecedent is unverifiable and is not claimed.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Chain node had oil weakening — 'the premium is leaking', with 81 named as the level that breaks it</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Crude printed 81.06, did not close under it, and rallied to 86.45.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The node was wrong, and its own invalidation line is what caught it — in hours, rather than at the end of the week. Flipped to strengthening on the tape rather than on narrative. Worth keeping on the board precisely because it is the case that justifies writing the breaking condition down every time.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--critical)">✕</i>Missed</span></div></div><details class="fwork frest"><summary>21 older resolved calls, 20 Jul to 31 Jul — 15 hit, 4 partial, 2 missed. All of them are counted in the tiles and the rate above, and the filter buttons search the whole ledger. This is the default view, not a shorter record.</summary><div class="fwork-b"><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Fade RTY strength into 2,971 unless the long end eases first</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>RTY topped at 2,976.30 overnight, through the level, and the long end never eased — the 10-year went to 4.735, the top of its range, and is still there.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Faded to 2,928.50, 47.8 points off the high. The condition attached to the call is what made it tradeable: the long end was the thing to watch, and it never gave the signal to stand down. The UoM leg at 22:00 HKT is not yet visible in the delayed bars, but it can only add to this.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Month-end: the 19:50Z imbalance is the largest non-news move of the session</div><div class="fdate">31 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Right about which event, wrong about what kind of event.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The 19:55Z bar traded 21,127 contracts against 1,994 in the bar before it — a factor of ten, and roughly three-quarters of its entire half-hour's volume inside five minutes. Only two half-hours in the whole cash session traded more than the 19:30–20:00 window, and both were the opening and ECI shock, so on VOLUME the call is exactly and unambiguously right: it was the largest non-news event of the session. On PRICE it was nothing — 8.80 points of range, eighth of fourteen half-hours, and a net move of one point down. The claim said move. Month-end imbalances are volume events, not price events, and the correct forecast is that a large quantity gets filled rather than that the price goes somewhere. Scored partial because the identification was right and the forecast quantity was the wrong quantity.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--warning)">◐</i>Partial</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Corrected chain rule — small caps cannot lead while the long end is under pressure</div><div class="fdate">30 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Tested on an UP day for the first time. NQ +2.34% and RTY +0.82% with the 30-yr pinned near 5.24%.</summary><div class="fwork-b">The rule was rewritten on Tuesday after it cost money; it has now paid in both directions.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Three-scenario FOMC map: hold+dovish 30 / hold+hawkish 40 / hike 30</div><div class="fdate">29 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Branch B resolved — hold with a hawkish September, and three members wanted a hike.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Right branch at the modal weight. But branch B said 'back below 7,400 cash' and stopped there; it did not anticipate that Microsoft's Azure print would overwhelm the macro within 24 hours. Correct on the Fed, incomplete on what else was in the week.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--warning)">◐</i>Partial</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">ES shelf third approach from below — knocking, not through</div><div class="fdate">29 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Never reclaimed. Cash fell from 7,444.88 to ~7,316 before Thursday's rally recovered it only to 7,411 — still under the 7,457 cash-equivalent.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Three approaches, three rejections.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY the cleanest structure — long above 2,957.4 targeting 2,968.5</div><div class="fdate">28 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Opened 2,960.40, closed 2,950.90. Faded all session.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Cause identified and the framework rule was rewritten: rate sensitivity outranks sector composition.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--critical)">✕</i>Missed</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">2,941 was a liquidity sweep of the 2,940 shelf, not a breakdown</div><div class="fdate">28 Jul</div><div class="fout">Correct that it held and reclaimed. Wrong that it would propel — it held and went nowhere.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--warning)">◐</i>Partial</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Rotation open, not liquidation — read the dispersion not the level</div><div class="fdate">28 Jul</div><div class="fout">Dow +1.20%, VIX -3.16%. No de-grossing. Exactly a sector rotation.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Don't chase NQ shorts into the bell — first bounce lives there</div><div class="fdate">28 Jul</div><div class="fout">Nasdaq closed +0.08% after Kospi -11%. Chasing the gap would have been wrong.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Chain amendment: oil ↓ moved the hike to September, didn't cancel it</div><div class="fdate">28 Jul</div><div class="fout">Monday: crude -8%, S&amp;P +0.02%. The transmission break was called before the close confirmed it.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">2,982.4 (61.8% fib) is the gate — sweep-reject risk there</div><div class="fdate">27 Jul</div><div class="fout">Topped at 2,981.9 and rejected. Called in real time on the chart.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY leads the relief leg</div><div class="fdate">27 Jul</div><div class="fout">RTY +0.87% vs ES +0.73% during Asia.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">'Hollow relief' detector — if RTY and breadth don't confirm, expect a pre-FOMC fade</div><div class="fdate">27 Jul</div><div class="fout">Fired correctly. The fade arrived on schedule.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Oil blow-off warning at $97–99 — watch for the reversal</div><div class="fdate">24 Jul</div><div class="fout">Brent kissed $102 then gave back ~$15 in three sessions.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">NQ is the weak leg, not RTY — don't autopilot the small-cap short</div><div class="fdate">24 Jul</div><div class="fout">Week of 20–24 Jul: NQ -2.1% vs RTY -0.3%.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">ES below the shelf targets 7,493 in your frame</div><div class="fdate">23 Jul</div><div class="fout">Hit in full.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY 3,000-wall rejection short</div><div class="fdate">22 Jul</div><div class="fout">Rejected and traded down as mapped.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Intel: the market pays sellers of AI compute</div><div class="fdate">23 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Right that it was conditional and that SAP/Oracle/ServiceNow held gains.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Wrong-footed by the -6.5% reversal after a blowout beat — that reversal became the 'distribution into strength' lesson.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--warning)">◐</i>Partial</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Post-opex week weakness prior</div><div class="fdate">21 Jul</div><div class="fout">S&amp;P -0.6% on the week. Prior scored.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">Turnaround Tuesday prior</div><div class="fdate">21 Jul</div><div class="fout">Squeeze delivered on schedule.</div></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--good)">✓</i>Hit</span></div></div><div class="frow"><div><div class="fcall">RTY levels from Investing.com futures page (~2,738 pivot cluster)</div><div class="fdate">20 Jul</div><details class="fwork"><summary>Data was ~8% wrong — actual RTY was ~2,970.</summary><div class="fwork-b">Levels voided the same day; all RTY levels now cross-checked against cash Russell 2000. This one is a process failure, and it stays on the board.</div></details></div><div><span class="chip"><i style="color:var(--critical)">✕</i>Missed</span></div></div></div></details></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- TRADE PLAN — the actionable summary. Requested in the original seven-part
     brief ("an advisory section on how to prepare trades for the day") and
     scattered until now across scenario invalids and the decision board.
     One entry per actionable decision, INCLUDING the no-trades — a stated
     no-trade with its reasons is a decision, and leaving it off this card
     would make the card look like it always wants you in something. -->
<section class="card" data-feeds="ES,RTY" id="rail-trade-plan">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Trade plan</div>
    <div class="card-sub" id="planSub">2 decisions from the last brief — 2 actionable, 0 deliberate no-trades. The no-trades are decisions too; a plan that only lists entries always wants you in something.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body"><div id="planList"><div class="plan"><div class="plan-hd"><span class="plan-id">ES sweep-reclaim</span><span class="plan-dir long">LONG</span><span class="plan-st waiting">WAITING</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Setup</span><span>Sweep of a session extreme that fails to continue</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">When</span><span>The first ninety minutes after the US cash open, EVERY trading day. The window is derived from 09:30 America/New_York through the tz database, so it follows the DST changes rather than being retyped around them.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Judged leg</span><span>Long bias while the higher timeframe is bullish AND the consolidation break to new highs holds. The entry is not the breakout — it is the discount a failed sweep offers after one.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Trigger</span><span>NO FIXED TRIGGER PRICE, and this is a property of the setup rather than a gap in the entry. The condition is a SEQUENCE: a sweep BELOW a key level — an hourly or daily extreme, or the Asia, London or NY session low — that then FAILS TO CONTINUE, entered on the failure rather than on the sweep. WHICH level is not knowable in advance; it is whichever extreme the open reaches for. Read the candidates off the ladder each session rather than from this line: a price typed here would be a guess wearing a trigger’s clothes, and the first sweep of a DIFFERENT low would leave the card describing a trade nobody took.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Entry</span><span>On the failure to continue, not on the sweep. The stop belongs under the sweep’s own low, which does not exist until it prints, so no stop price is stated here rather than a number being invented for one.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Breaks it</span><span>THE STANDING BIAS IS LIVE WHILE THE CONSOLIDATION BREAK HOLDS, and “no longer holds” means three checkable things, any one of which retires this entry until it is re-argued: (1) a DAILY close back beneath the point of control of the range price broke out of — the liquidity map publishes the POC every bake, so this is read rather than remembered; (2) spot back INSIDE the 70% value area low of that same range, which is the structural statement of “the breakout was reclaimed”; (3) a new swing low on the daily beneath the prior one, which is the higher timeframe itself turning. Until one of those is true, a failed sweep is a discount; after one of them, the same pattern is a lower high in a downtrend and this entry does not describe it.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Source</span><span>Standing — baseline setup, last reviewed 27 Aug</span></div></div><div class="plan"><div class="plan-hd"><span class="plan-id">RTY sweep-reclaim</span><span class="plan-dir long">LONG</span><span class="plan-st waiting">WAITING</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Setup</span><span>Sweep of a session extreme that fails to continue</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">When</span><span>The same ninety minutes as ES, every trading day. RTY is the higher-beta expression of one idea, not a second trade — taking both is one position at double size.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Judged leg</span><span>Same read as ES. RTY typically sits harder against its own window high, which makes the sweep shallower in points and not necessarily shallower in pace terms — read the distance in x t30 rather than in points.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Trigger</span><span>NO FIXED TRIGGER PRICE, as ES. Same sequence: a sweep below an hourly or daily extreme, or an Asia, London or NY session low, that fails to continue. On RTY the candidates are frequently inside a single half-hour’s normal travel of spot, so check the distance in x t30 before treating a sweep as information — a level swept from one tick away is noise wearing the shape of a signal.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Entry</span><span>On the failure to continue. Stop under the sweep low once it exists.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Breaks it</span><span>THE SAME THREE TESTS AS ES, read against RTY’s own profile: a daily close beneath the point of control of the range it broke from, spot back inside that range’s 70% value area low, or a lower swing low on the daily. RTY turning while ES holds is not by itself an invalidation of this entry — it is the cross-index divergence the first confirmation asks about, and it argues for taking the ES expression rather than for standing down.</span></div><div class="plan-row"><span class="plan-k">Source</span><span>Standing — baseline setup, last reviewed 27 Aug</span></div></div></div>
  <div class="plan-foot">Every condition here is stated as a CLOSE where it is a
  close, and the chips now carry two grades of evidence and say which. When the
  live bars cover an entry’s stated window, the chip reads the actual
  thirty-minute closes inside it — “invalidated at 13:00Z” is
  an observation, timestamped from the same bars the charts use, and a chip
  whose window has passed says WINDOW CLOSED with what happened inside it. When
  the bars cannot answer — before the first fetch, or past their eight-hour
  reach — the chip falls back to the trailing-day range, which can only
  see whether a level TRADED, never whether a bar closed through it: a chip
  reading “close unverified” is an instruction to check your
  platform’s bar, not a verdict. Set at the last brief; prices refresh
  live.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- POSITION SIZING. The card the whole page was preparing a decision for and
     then declined to help execute: everything above knows the level, the
     invalidation, the measured pace of the session and the typical excursion,
     and none of it knew how big anything is traded. Arithmetic only — it sizes
     the risk YOU state against the stop YOU state, and refuses to imply that a
     number it computed is a trade worth taking. -->
<section class="card" data-rail="Sizing" data-feeds="ES,RTY,NQ,YM" id="rail-sizing">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Position sizing</div>
    <div class="card-sub">What your own stop is worth in contracts and in money, and whether that stop is wide enough to survive the session you are actually in. Nothing here is stored — the numbers live for as long as the page is open.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div class="sz-form">
      <div class="sz-f"><label for="szEq">Account</label>
        <input id="szEq" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="equity" value=""></div>
      <div class="sz-f"><label for="szEqCcy">Account in</label>
        <select id="szEqCcy"><option value="US$">US$</option><option value="HK$">HK$</option></select></div>
      <div class="sz-f"><label for="szRisk">Risk %</label>
        <input id="szRisk" type="number" step="0.05" inputmode="decimal" value="1"></div>
      <div class="sz-f"><label for="szInst">Contract</label>
        <select id="szInst"><option value="ES" selected="">ES  · E-mini S&amp;P 500</option><option value="MES">MES · Micro E-mini S&amp;P</option><option value="RTY">RTY · E-mini Russell 2000</option><option value="M2K">M2K · Micro Russell 2000</option><option value="NQ">NQ  · E-mini Nasdaq 100</option><option value="MNQ">MNQ · Micro Nasdaq 100</option><option value="YM">YM  · E-mini Dow</option><option value="MYM">MYM · Micro Dow</option><option value="MHI">MHI · Mini Hang Seng</option></select></div>
      <div class="sz-f"><label for="szStop">Stop (pts)</label>
        <input id="szStop" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="distance" value=""></div>
    </div>
    <div class="sz-props" id="szProps"><button class="sz-prop" type="button" data-d="6.7500"><b>6.75</b> · 1× the typical 30-minute move in this session</button><button class="sz-prop" type="button" data-d="10.1250"><b>10.13</b> · 1.5× that move — clear of ordinary breathing</button></div>
    <div class="sz-out" id="szOut"></div>
    <div class="sz-note" id="szNote">Enter an account size, the share of it you are willing to lose on this trade, and your stop distance in points. The chips above are stop distances this page can measure — they are offered, not chosen for you.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- TRADE JOURNAL
     The scorecard above grades the ANALYSIS: whether a call this page made came
     good. Nothing on the board records what was actually DONE, and those are
     different questions — a board can be right about a level all week while the
     account bleeds out in a session nobody looked at.

     NOTHING PERSISTS, and the card says so on its face rather than letting a
     reader find out by closing a tab. Browser storage stays out by the same
     standing rule that keeps it out of the sizing card: a number that survives
     silently is a number that can go stale invisibly, and this board's whole
     claim is that every figure on it is measured now. What it offers instead is
     an EXPORT — one block to copy, which pastes back into the next brief and is
     baked into the page the way PLAN and CALLS already are. -->
<section class="card" data-rail="Journal" data-feeds="ES,RTY,NQ,YM" id="rail-journal">
  <div class="card-head">
    <div class="card-title">Trade journal</div>
    <div class="card-sub">What was actually done, and what each session is worth. The scorecard grades the analysis; this grades the trading. Nothing is stored — entries live while the page is open, and the export below is how they survive.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="card-body">
    <div class="jr-form">
      <div class="jr-f"><label for="jrInst">Contract</label><select id="jrInst"><option value="ES">ES  · E-mini S&amp;P 500</option><option value="MES">MES · Micro E-mini S&amp;P</option><option value="RTY">RTY · E-mini Russell 2000</option><option value="M2K">M2K · Micro Russell 2000</option><option value="NQ">NQ  · E-mini Nasdaq 100</option><option value="MNQ">MNQ · Micro Nasdaq 100</option><option value="YM">YM  · E-mini Dow</option><option value="MYM">MYM · Micro Dow</option><option value="MHI">MHI · Mini Hang Seng</option></select></div>
      <div class="jr-f"><label for="jrDir">Side</label><select id="jrDir"><option value="long">Long</option><option value="short">Short</option></select></div>
      <div class="jr-f"><label for="jrIn">Entry</label><input id="jrIn" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="price" value=""></div>
      <div class="jr-f"><label for="jrOut">Exit</label><input id="jrOut" type="number" step="any" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="price" value=""></div>
      <div class="jr-f"><label for="jrQty">Contracts</label><input id="jrQty" type="number" step="1" inputmode="numeric" value="1"></div>
      <div class="jr-f jr-wide"><label for="jrNote">Note</label><input id="jrNote" type="text" placeholder="why, in your own words" value=""></div>
    </div>
    <div class="jr-btns">
      <button class="jr-btn" id="jrAdd" type="button">Record it</button>
      <button class="jr-btn ghost" id="jrExport" type="button">Export</button>
      <button class="jr-btn ghost" id="jrClear" type="button">Clear</button>
    </div>
    <div class="jr-body" id="jrBody"><div class="jr-note">No trades recorded in this session. Record one above and the by-session split appears here. <b>Nothing is stored</b> — use Export before you close the page, and paste that block into the next brief to have it baked in.</div></div>
    <textarea class="jr-out" id="jrOutBox" readonly=""></textarea>
  </div>
</section>

</div><!-- /deck -->

<footer class="foot">
  Informational analysis, not financial advice.<br>
  Built for Walton · levels re-anchor to your platform · v1 static-snapshot build
</footer>

</div>
</div>

<script>
(function(){
"use strict";

/* ==========================================================
   DATA BLOCK — this is the only part that changes daily.
   When the WaFT relay is live, `px` and `chg` get overwritten
   from data/latest/quotes.json instead of being typed in.
   ========================================================== */

/* AS_OF is the sentence at the top of the page, and it is the one line here that
   can invalidate everything below it without touching a single number. Two
   incidents shaped how it is written and how it is now checked.

   31 Jul. The tiles read ES 7,442.50 / RTY 2,936.90 and were labelled "Thursday's
   close". They were not — they were the 16:30Z print, three and a half hours
   before the bell, and every figure derived from them was out by roughly thirty ES
   points. The fix was to stop trusting a single source: the archive's 19:55Z bar
   carries the 4pm ET auction's volume spike (99,704 ES contracts against ~10,000
   around it) and the feed's own prev_close is written by an unrelated source, and
   the two agreed to within a tick. Four numbers from three sources agreeing is
   verification. The earlier figure agreed with nothing and was published anyway.

   3 Aug. The opposite failure, and the worse one. The line still read "Thu 30 Jul
   close" on a Monday morning, sixty open-market hours later, while the payload
   underneath it was fresh. Every suite passed, because every suite compared the
   page to itself. agegate.mjs now parses this string, extracts the date and the
   weekday word, and refuses the bake unless both agree with the snapshot's own
   clock — which means this line can no longer quietly outlive the data it labels.

   So: name the moment the data was taken, and say which parts of the board are
   live and which are a stale session's close. Both halves are load-bearing. */
var AS_OF = "Wed 26 Aug, Hong Kong&rsquo;s afternoon and the US futures overnight &mdash; and this page is THREE VINTAGES, so read it in three parts. THE LEVELS AND THE STRUCTURE ARE TONIGHT&rsquo;S: the snapshot price, the liquidity map, the killzones, the day shape, reach, the tape, the curve and the ladder were all re-cut from an archive that had grown by thirteen thousand commits since this page was last baked, and the trade plan was written against them. THE PUBLIC PAGE IS OLDER THAN EVERYTHING ELSE HERE, which is the first thing to know about everything below: the droplet fetches this source every fifteen minutes and has it, but the file GitHub Pages serves &mdash; the only one a public link reaches &mdash; has not been written since 13 August, so any figure you remember from a public link was that old. Nothing had ever published it; a twelfth build gate now refuses to call a bake complete while it is behind, and a second cron line installs the baked page by the same route the source already takes. THREE CARDS STILL COULD NOT BE REGENERATED and are carried forward from 13 August &mdash; cross-market agreement, the catalyst board and the dollar &mdash; because the input their generator needs no longer exists; each of the three states the date it was measured on its own face rather than leaving you to infer it from this line. THE HK CONTRACT ROLLED TONIGHT: MHI Aug-26 dies on 28 August, so the board now describes Sep-26, and it carries NO BASIS at all &mdash; August&rsquo;s figure belonged to August&rsquo;s contract and is not carried over, and no September measurement exists to replace it. Convert HK levels by hand until one does. THE MARKET ARGUMENT IS OLDER THAN EITHER: the scenario prose has been checked against tonight&rsquo;s profile and every claim the measurement no longer supports has been WITHDRAWN &mdash; including three RTY shelves that have consolidated into a single pool over the eleven days &mdash; but it has not been re-argued. So read the levels as current, the three dated cards as their own dates, the HK board as a contract without a basis, and the argument as evidence-checked rather than fresh.";

/* TILES — the five headline cards.

   These carry JUDGEMENT ONLY. px and chg are null on purpose: a hand-typed
   headline price is a number that was true when it was written and is silently
   false after the next refresh, which is the single most visible way this page
   can lie. So the tile supplies label / bias / state / note, and the price is
   read out of SNAPSHOT at paint time and overwritten again by the live feed.

   A literal in px still wins if one is ever needed — the override survives —
   but agegate refuses to bake a literal that disagrees with the payload sitting
   underneath it, so an override can be stale by at most the tolerance.

   The note is the part to write carefully. It should say something that stays
   true across a refresh: a level, a structural fact, a data-quality warning.
   A note that restates the percentage printed beside it is wasted space and
   goes stale within the hour. */
var TILES = [
  { k:"ES",  label:"ES (futures)",  px:null, chg:null, bias:"Parked on a shelf",  st:"warn", nochg:true,
    /* Rewritten 3 Aug, and the reason is a gap in the gates worth stating. This
       read "Above the window / spot is over the 4-day profile's top — nothing
       measured overhead". Both halves went false on one refresh: the window grew
       a top above spot, and a 24-turn pool formed between the two. Nothing
       caught it, because the sentence named no price — prose_levels checks the
       levels a claim quotes, and a claim that quotes none is invisible to it.
       A qualitative bias line is the cheapest place on this page for a stale
       assertion to hide, which is an argument for putting a number in every
       one of them rather than for trusting the ones that have no number.

       Rewritten again 4 Aug, and the reason is worth more than the rewrite. The
       sentence this replaces was RIGHT — it named a pool on the market at the
       window top and called anything past it unmapped. Price went through it,
       travelled eighty-three points, and stopped where a new pool built. So the
       tile is now saying the identical thing one shelf higher, which is the
       first evidence this board has that "unmapped" is a forecast about the
       MAP rather than a hedge: the map could not price the next stop and did
       not pretend to, and the next stop was a level that did not exist yet. */
    note:"an eleven-bin shelf runs 7,760.00 to 7,785.00 and it is the densest stretch on the ES map, 23.7% of the window inside eleven bins of a hundred and eighty-nine. ||| What the shelf carries has now changed four times: none at all when this tile was first written, then two straddling price, then a single one heavier than both of those together, then that same one thinning, then thickening while price walked away &mdash; then, at the payrolls print, taken whole; rebuilt within the hour as price camped on it; then taken a SECOND time by the afternoon walk to the close. {{pool ES 7764.68}} has since rebuilt a third time and now carries {{turns ES 7764.68}} turns, heavier than either earlier rebuild &mdash; a SIXTH state of one level: emptied, defended-and-losing, consumed, rebuilt, consumed again, rebuilt heavier. What the day left instead is the pair around it, and the reopen has been feeding the upper one all night. Well beneath, {{gone ES 7755.66}} no longer carries a pool at all &mdash; that structure has left the map, and the nearest survivor is four bins away and is the level this row has just described, so it is left withdrawn rather than re-pointed onto a price the paragraph already owns. OVERTAKEN &mdash; what the Asian session built here was withdrawn when it left the map, and refusing to re-point it at the cluster then sitting 1.25 bins away was right at the time. <b>{{pool ES 7784.43}}</b> is where that cluster keys now, on {{turns ES 7784.43}} turns &mdash; and the distance between it and the price this row withdrew has been widening since the call was made. The rule the withdrawal was defending still stands &mdash; quietly moving a sentence onto a neighbouring object is how a claim stops meaning what it was written to mean. The token above hard-fails if the pool re-forms there, so this withdrawal cannot outlive its own truth. Touched within the hour. Friday&rsquo;s cash session closed between the two, at 7,778.50, and Monday&rsquo;s Asian hours have spent themselves building the upper edge rather than testing the lower. It sits under the cash point of control, which Wednesday moved to 7,772.50, so the price the session kept turning at and the price it spent the most time at are the same price, which is not usually true and is most of why this level is worth a tile. A pool is a different object from the bin that merely holds the most volume: it is where the session turned, not just where it sat. Here they coincide. What has changed is the side, three times in one day. The trailing session has run 7,725.50 to 7,786.75; spot spent the European morning thirty points under the shelf&rsquo;s floor, the payrolls half-hour carried it back inside, the midday fade pressed the floor from beneath &mdash; and the afternoon settled it: a cash close at 7,778.50, inside the shelf, after tagging 7,786.75 through its top. The overhead resistance this tile described was real twice, and it was bought through whole twice. <b>{{gone ES 7820.25}}</b> is no longer a cluster of any kind, though it is still the top of the whole measurement: the two turns printed there have left the map &mdash; WITHDRAWN rather than re-pointed at the cluster above it, <b>{{pool ES 7824.50}}</b> on {{turns ES 7824.50}} turns, which is plainly not the two-turn object this tile described. It is why spot came off it without pausing anywhere on the way down. Below, the map is hollow down to 7,636.42; price fell into that hollow on Thursday and has climbed back out, so the hollow is underneath price again rather than around it." },
  { k:"RTY", label:"RTY (futures)", px:null, chg:null, bias:"Shelf, then a hole",  st:"warn", nochg:true,
    /* Rewritten 3 Aug. It read "at 2,950 — the heaviest pool on the board, 2,941
       in cash", which parses as a claim that 2,950 is the heaviest pool. It is
       not, and the three numbers are three different measurements: 2,950.02 is
       the nearest STOP POOL (10 turns, round number), 2,936 is the heaviest
       all-hours VOLUME BIN at 4.01%, and 2,941 is the CASH point of control at
       3.23%. Each label now travels with its number.

       Read that paragraph as of the moment it was written. Every figure in it
       has since moved at least once — the pool's price, its turn count, both
       percentages — and an earlier version of this comment tried to keep up by
       restating the new ones, which lasted exactly one refresh before going
       stale again. So it no longer quotes any: the paragraph above records a
       DISTINCTION that stays true (three labels, three different measurements,
       each travelling with its own number) and the note below carries the
       current values, where the checker can see them.

       That split is deliberate and it is the point of the whole comment.
       prose_levels.py quotes this paragraph as the invariant it was built to
       enforce — and this paragraph is the one piece of text in the block it
       cannot read, because it blanks source comments out on purpose: a note
       explaining a decision is a record of a decision, and a record may quote a
       measurement as it stood. Which is also exactly how figures up here drifted
       for a day with every gate green. The rule's own source text sits outside
       the rule's jurisdiction. The defence is not to check the comment, it is to
       keep numbers out of it — nothing structural gets asserted up here that is
       not also asserted in the note below, where the check can see it, so a
       drifted comment costs a reader a stale anecdote and never a stale level.

       Status went warn to crit on 4 Aug and not because anything is wrong with
       the data. This tile and the ES one now describe opposite structures at the
       same moment — both contracts at the top of their windows, one standing on
       the heaviest thing it has built all week and the other standing on the
       lightest bin in ninety-two — and that difference is the most decision-
       relevant thing on the board for someone deciding where to get back in.
       The colour is carrying that, not a data fault. */
    note:"an eight-bin shelf runs 3,043.00 to 3,050.00 and spot is BENEATH it, which inverts what the shelf is for: it stops being the floor price rests on and becomes supply price has to climb back through, and 3,043.00 is that line. ||| Overhead the shelf edge <b>{{gone RTY 3046.45}}</b> is WITHDRAWN &mdash; nothing is clustered there now. It did not move: over the eleven days since this row was last measured, it and the shelves beneath it were consumed by a single object that formed across the band they occupied, <b>{{pool RTY 3038.54}}</b> at {{turns RTY 3038.54}} turns. The heavier structure that stood at the top of this shelf a week ago is no longer on the map at all, and price never went back to test it &mdash; it was not consumed, it dissolved as the clustering re-formed lower. What the map holds now is beneath: Monday and Tuesday between them dealt every price from 3,016.00 up to 3,045.80, which are the two sessions&rsquo; extremes and not levels. What they left is three heavyweight clusters inside the hollow this row used to call empty &mdash; {{pool RTY 3029.48}} and {{pool RTY 3022.05}} &mdash; carrying {{turns RTY 3029.48}} and {{turns RTY 3022.05}} turns respectively &mdash; and a third that is not there any more: <b>{{gone RTY 3036.52}}</b> is WITHDRAWN, with no cluster standing there now, its weight having been absorbed into {{pool RTY 3038.54}} above. The hollow did not empty. It became the range, and the shelf above it is now the only thing on this side of the map that price has not already dealt in." },
  { k:"HSI", label:"HSI (proxy)",   px:null, chg:null, bias:"Level only",        st:"crit", nochg:true,
    note:"prior close froze after Thursday and thawed to the wrong number — it is still wrong on the new vendor, so the fault was never the vendor" },
  { k:"WTI", label:"WTI crude",     px:null, chg:null, bias:"Trigger fired",     st:"crit", nochg:true,
    note:"the only reference still frozen — read the level: 81.51 and 81 both went on Sunday's reopen in one six-minute gap, not on Friday; 80 has traded both sides since" },
  { k:"VIX", label:"VIX (cash)",    px:null, chg:null, bias:"Level only",        st:"crit", nochg:true,
    note:"cash, and the percentage is suppressed — the reference this tile measured from was wrong in sign, printing a fall on a day vol rose; the level is sound, the change is not" }
];

/* One map, two consumers. renderTiles() runs at first paint and paintTiles()
   runs on every feed tick; both need it, and it lives up here because var
   hoists the declaration but not the assignment — declared beside paintTiles
   it would be undefined at first paint and take the whole tile strip out. */
var TILE_MAP = { ES:"ES", RTY:"RTY", HSI:"HSI", WTI:"CRUDE", VIX:"VIX" };

/* Quotes whose PUBLISHED PERCENTAGE is measured against a price the instrument
   never traded from, so every figure derived from it spans the wrong interval.

   This was called FROZEN_PREV until gold joined it, and the rename is the point.
   "Frozen" named a CAUSE — the relay's two CNBC legs, HSI and crude, carry a
   prev_close that stopped rolling after Thursday, so both percentages are
   two-session numbers wearing a one-session label. Gold's reference is not
   frozen. It rolled at the Sunday reopen, from 4100.10 to 4049.10, DOWNWARD, to
   a number below the close it was replacing and 58 points below gold's own last
   Friday print. Same consequence, different cause, and a list named after one
   cause reads as an argument that the other does not belong on it.

   What the entries have in common is the only thing that matters to a consumer:
   the denominator is not the last price the market traded, so the numerator is
   not this session's move.

   The gold entry is worth writing down in full because of how long it survived.
   agegate's section 6 flagged it the day that check was written and printed the
   flag on every build afterwards. Nothing acted, because section 6's HARD failure
   only fires when a flagged quote sits on a tile still printing its percentage,
   and gold has no tile. It has something more consequential: it is one of the
   three legs of regime(), at 20% of the composite. The guard found it, said so
   out loud, and the page went on scoring a number the guard had already refused
   to vouch for. A warning nobody is obliged to answer is a warning that will be
   read as decoration — which is why UNTRUSTED_PREV and PREV_OK below are now
   both mandatory, and a flagged quote on neither list fails the bake.

   Removed — not edited around — the moment the upstream reference is fixed. */
var PREV_GAP_TOL = 1.5;   /* per cent between prev_close and the session open */

/* Whatever an instrument did overnight, its session OPEN is by construction near
   the price it closed at. That is not a model, it is what "previous close" means.
   A gap of a few tenths is ordinary; a gap of several per cent means one of the
   two numbers belongs to a different day.

   Returns null rather than 0 when the payload cannot answer — an instrument with
   no overnight session has no on_open, and guessing one is how a check starts
   inventing the thing it is supposed to be measuring. */
function prevGap(q){
  if (!q) return null;
  var o = q.on_open, p = q.prev_close;
  if (typeof o !== "number" || !isFinite(o)) return null;
  if (typeof p !== "number" || !isFinite(p) || !p) return null;
  return (o - p) / p * 100;
}

/* PREV_GAP_TOL asks how far the reference sits from the open in per cent, which is
   the right question for an instrument that gapped. It is the wrong question for one
   that is merely a session stale, because how far a stale reference lands depends on
   what the market did that day rather than on how broken the field is. On 4 August it
   let two of the four index futures through: ES at 1.45% and YM at 1.33% sat under the
   1.5% line carrying precisely the defect RTY and NQ were failed for. A threshold that
   decides which of four identical faults gets published is not measuring the fault.

   This asks the scale-free version instead — how many of THIS session's own ranges away
   the reference sits. A price that opens where it closed sits well under one. A
   reference belonging to a different session sits several, because the distance it has
   to explain is a whole day's move while the range it is measured against is a single
   overnight. On the payload that prompted this, the four index futures read 9.7, 6.2,
   6.1 and 5.1 while everything else on the board read 4.1 or less. That the four which
   separated are exactly the four index futures is better evidence than the cut itself.

   4.5 is a judgement, not a measurement, and it is stated as one. NQ at 5.1 sits near
   it, so this predicate is expected to stop firing before the defect does — and agegate
   reporting an entry that no longer fires is the designed way to discover that, rather
   than a surprise. It cannot separate a stale reference from a real weekend gap either;
   nothing inside one payload can, which is what PREV_OK exists for. The JUDGEMENT that
   these four are stale was made against the archive and is written into each why below.
   The predicate only asks whether the condition that judgement was made about is still
   in the payload. */
var STALE_REF_RANGES = 4.5;
function prevGapRanges(q){
  if (!q) return null;
  var o = q.on_open, p = q.prev_close, r = q.on_range;
  if (typeof o !== "number" || !isFinite(o)) return null;
  if (typeof p !== "number" || !isFinite(p) || !p) return null;
  if (typeof r !== "number" || !isFinite(r) || r <= 0) return null;
  return Math.abs(o - p) / r;
}

var UNTRUSTED_PREV = {
  /* holds:null means the defect is invisible from inside a single payload, so no
     predicate is possible and none is faked. That is the true state of all three.
     HSI and CRUDE were listed when their prev_close FROZE — it stopped rolling
     after Thursday — and a reference that has stopped moving looks exactly like one
     that moved correctly. The only way to see it is to watch the field fail to change
     across sessions, which a page holding one payload at a time cannot do. So these
     stand until a human takes them down, and the guard says so rather than
     pretending to check.

     CRUDE's has since rolled, to 84.67, and that did not settle the question, it
     sharpened it. The archive has this source publishing 86.80 as the weekend price
     for thirty-one hours and then replacing it with 84.67 at 04:00Z on Sunday,
     before any reopen. Both cannot be Friday's close. 84.67 is the more defensible
     of the two, being about where the regular session ended — but a percentage
     measured from a number the publisher itself revised mid-weekend is not one to
     print, and which of the two is the settle is not a question one payload can
     answer either. What changes is the entry's REASON, not its disposition, and
     the reason is worth rewriting rather than leaving to rot: an entry that gives
     a stale cause invites the next reader to check the cause, find it false, and
     take the entry down for the wrong reason.

     HSI's reference has since started rolling again, and that is exactly why the
     entry stays. This payload carries 25884.43 against a session that opened
     0.40% above it, which is what a working previous close looks like. But the
     defect was never that one payload read wrong — it was that no payload can
     tell. One payload that looks right is one observation of the thing that was
     already unobservable, and a fault that comes and goes is worse than one that
     stays, because the good readings are the ones that get trusted. Taking this
     down needs the field watched ACROSS sessions, which is a relay-side job.

     One correction to both entries, and it is not cosmetic. Each used to open
     with "cnbc path", naming the vendor the defect was seen on. That prefix is
     now false: this payload carries src "yahoo" for CRUDE, ES, RTY and HSI, and
     only VIX still says cnbc. The vendor changed underneath two live data-quality
     entries without either of them noticing, which is worth more attention than
     the wording it broke. A frozen reference observed on one publisher tells you
     nothing about the next publisher's reference — so the OBSERVATION that put
     these here is now historical, while the CLAIM they make (one payload cannot
     tell you a rolling field is fixed) is a property of the page and survives any
     vendor. That is why they stand rather than being taken down and re-earned:
     the reasoning never depended on who was publishing. It does mean the clock
     on both restarts, because the evidence for the underlying fault is now about
     a source this page has stopped reading.

     One further correction, and it cuts against the paragraph above. HSI's entry
     said the source changing to yahoo restarted its clock. It did not. This
     payload carries HSI on src yahoo and the prev_close is STILL wrong — 25858.88
     against a true Friday close of 25884.43, which HK press confirms independently
     by reporting Monday at plus 0.48%. A defect that survives the vendor swap was
     never the vendor's. The clock does not restart on an entry whose fault has
     just been shown to be structural, so the wording below is corrected rather
     than the entry re-earned.

     VIX joins them on different evidence, and this one is provable from inside the
     page. The baked snapshot published VIX at minus 5.91% off a prev_close of
     17.09. The page's own news entry for 31 July records Friday's close as 15.99,
     and from 15.99 the snapshot px of 16.07 is plus 0.50% — so the published
     figure was wrong in SIGN, not merely in size, and a volatility tile that
     paints green on a day vol rose is the one error a vol reading must not make.
     The live payload now carries 15.99 and is correct. That is precisely the
     freeze-and-thaw shape the entries above describe, arriving on the last source
     still publishing as cnbc, and precisely why one payload cannot settle it: the
     good reading is the one that gets trusted. Suggestive of the vendor, not proof
     of it — one instrument is not a sample. holds:null for the same reason as the
     others. */
  HSI:   { why:"prev_close froze after Thursday, thawed wrong — this payload is on the yahoo path and still publishes 25858.88 against a true Friday close of 25884.43, so the defect outlived the vendor swap and is structural; freeze/thaw is unobservable from one payload", holds:null },
  CRUDE: { why:"two Friday closes published — 86.80 held for 31h across Saturday, then replaced by 84.67 at 04:00Z Sunday, before any reopen; seen on the cnbc path, source now yahoo", holds:null },
  VIX:   { why:"published minus 5.91% off a prev_close of 17.09 while this page&rsquo;s own 31 Jul news entry records Friday&rsquo;s close as 15.99 — from 15.99 the same px of 16.07 is plus 0.50%, so the sign was wrong, not just the size; the live payload now carries 15.99 correctly, which is why one payload cannot settle it", holds:null },

  /* Gold is the opposite case, which is why it gets a predicate. Its defect is
     visible from inside the payload: the reference disagrees with the session open
     sitting beside it in the same object, by 2.03%. Three contradictions, any one
     sufficient — the payload's own on_open (4131.10) is ABOVE px (4121.60), so gold
     is down 0.23% overnight while the field claims +1.79%; the relay's Sunday
     payloads all carry Friday's final print at 4107.0, from which gold is +0.36%;
     and the reference rolled DOWNWARD past the close it was replacing.

     holds() does not re-derive that judgement — it could not, since a 2% gap is
     equally consistent with a real weekend gap, which is the whole reason PREV_OK
     exists. It asks the narrower question a machine can answer: is the condition I
     judged still in this payload? When upstream fixes the reference the gap closes,
     the entry loses its subject, and gold starts publishing again — which is the
     correct outcome, and which agegate then reports as an entry to delete rather
     than leaving it to rot in place. */
  /* GOLD, DELETED ON 7 AUGUST AND RESTORED THE SAME DAY. The deletion was wrong
     and the way it was wrong is worth more than the entry.

     agegate advised removal for three sessions on the ground that the predicate
     "no longer fires against this payload — the reference looks repaired". It
     was followed on the third asking. sentedge failed within the hour: its BAD
     GOLD REFERENCE case seeds a prev_close 2.03% from the open and expects the
     card to refuse the regime and name the leg at fault, and with this entry
     gone the card scored it as an ordinary "Inflation risk premium" instead.

     THE DISTINCTION agegate CANNOT MAKE. This table holds two kinds of entry
     and they go stale in opposite ways. HSI and CRUDE carry holds:null, meaning
     no predicate is possible, and those genuinely can outlive their condition
     with nothing to notice — a human has to retire them. GOLD carries a
     PREDICATE, and a predicate that is quiet is not a predicate that is dead:
     it is live protection standing over a reference that happens to be sound
     today. Advising its deletion reads "the guard is not firing" as "the guard
     is not needed", which is the same argument as removing a smoke alarm
     because the house is not currently on fire.

     Kept, therefore, and agegate has been taught the difference rather than
     merely disagreed with: it now only advises retiring an entry that has no
     predicate to fire. Original reason retained below, unedited. */
  GOLD:  { why:"prev_close 4049.10 is below both Friday's last print (4107.0) and the overnight open (4131.10)",
           holds:function(q){ var g = prevGap(q); return g != null && Math.abs(g) > PREV_GAP_TOL; } }

  /* ES, RTY, NQ and YM were here, on one measured defect: all four referenced to
     FRIDAY's settle rather than Monday's, publishing a full session of move as
     though it happened overnight -- about two per cent, against a real overnight
     of one to two tenths. Two readings implying opposite decisions for anyone
     deciding whether to re-enter, and the wrong one was the one on the page.

     TAKEN DOWN 5 Aug, on measurement rather than on the clock. The references
     have rolled: prevGapRanges() reads 0.045 on ES, 0.015 on RTY, 0.051 on NQ
     and 0.059 on YM, against STALE_REF_RANGES of 4.5. That is two orders of
     magnitude clear, not a marginal pass, which matters because the predicate
     was expected to stop firing before the defect stopped existing -- NQ sat at
     5.1 against a threshold of 4.5 and the comment above STALE_REF_RANGES says
     so in advance. A reading of 0.051 is not that case. The defect is gone, not
     merely under the line, and the entries are removed rather than edited around
     because that is what the header of this section requires.

     One thing learned on the way, recorded because it is the kind that recurs:
     the takedown was nearly argued from |px - prev_close| / on_range, which reads
     0.74 and 0.68 and would have been a marginal pass supporting the same
     conclusion for the wrong reason. prevGapRanges() uses on_open, not px, and
     has to -- the premise is that a session OPEN is by construction near the
     price it closed at, so any daylight is the reference being wrong, whereas px
     folds the whole overnight move into the numerator. The function was correct
     throughout. A page that publishes the right predicate is no protection at all
     against a reader computing a different one by hand beside it, and the only
     defence is to run the page's own function against the payload, which is what
     was done here. */
};

/* The other half, and the reason the guard can now be strict. A genuine weekend
   or event gap looks identical to a bad reference from inside the payload, and
   its percentage is CORRECT and should publish. Without somewhere to say that,
   the only way to quiet the detector would be to suppress a good number — so the
   guard would teach exactly the wrong habit.

   An entry here is a curated statement that the gap was checked and is real. It
   carries its reason for the same purpose the entries above do: so that the next
   person to read it can disagree with the judgement rather than guess at it. */
var PREV_OK = {
  /* empty today — no flagged quote has been cleared as a genuine gap */
};

/* The only reader of a change field on this page. Anything routed through here
   returns null rather than a wrong percentage, and null is already the "no data"
   path every consumer handles. fld is "chg_pct" everywhere except the 10-year,
   which the relay publishes in points as "chg".

   The finite check is written out longhand rather than calling num(). num() is
   declared INSIDE the live IIFE and this function is not, so calling it from
   here is a ReferenceError — and the first version of this function did exactly
   that. The cost was not a red error in the console, which is what makes it
   worth a comment: paintAll wraps its painters in a try/catch, so the throw was
   swallowed, and the two lines that set LIVEQ and LIVEFEED sit AFTER paintTone
   in that function and never ran. The visible symptom was a ladder that would
   not leave the baked snapshot price when a live payload arrived, four cards
   away from the code that broke. lnum() a few hundred lines below carries its
   own copy of this same guard for the same reason. */
/* The one place the lists are consulted, so the lists cannot be consulted two
   ways. Returns the curated reason when the quote's percentage must not publish,
   and null when it may — never a bare boolean, because every caller that suppresses
   a number owes the reader a sentence about why, and a boolean cannot carry one. */
function untrusted(q, key){
  var e = UNTRUSTED_PREV[key];
  if (!e) return null;
  if (!e.holds) return e.why;          /* not checkable from one payload; stands */
  return e.holds(q) ? e.why : null;    /* checkable, and checked against this one */
}

function liveChg(q, key, fld){
  if (!q) return null;
  if (untrusted(q, key)) return null;
  var v = q[fld || "chg_pct"];
  return (typeof v === "number" && isFinite(v)) ? v : null;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   CATALYSTS — the calendar, with the fields the board needs to say
   something beyond "this happens at this time".

     imp  1..3 impact. Ordinal, so it is drawn as bar HEIGHT, never a
          colour s